"""Authored deeper OS/database teaching with three distinct worked tasks per topic."""
from . import P, E

P('7-processes-syscalls','System calls',r'''
Think of a system call as crossing a guarded service boundary, rather than handing the processor to another application. The caller supplies an operation and arguments; the kernel checks authority and memory access, performs whatever service is currently possible, and reports an outcome. A successful return can describe partial progress, such as fewer bytes than requested, so applications must interpret the API contract rather than equating success with completion. To trace a call, separate privilege mode, process state, resource changes, and return value. Assume ordinary user/kernel protection and a stated blocking policy. A rejected request should not bypass validation merely because the user program supplied an address that looks numerically plausible.
''',
E('fundamental','A running user process calls getpid; the service completes immediately. Must a different process run?',"Enter the service::::The syscall entry transfers control to a protected kernel handler while retaining the caller's identity.~Complete the request::::The handler obtains the caller's process identifier without awaiting I/O.~Return to user mode::::The same process resumes after the call; there was a privilege transition but no required scheduling switch.",'No other process must run; a mode transition alone is sufficient.','The process remains the same before and after the call, so counting a newly scheduled process would add an unsupported event.'),
E('application','A blocking pipe contains three bytes and still has an open writer. A read requests eight bytes and returns available data immediately under the specified API. What happens to this read and the following read?','Consume available data::::The first call returns three bytes, which is a permitted short read rather than an error.~Update pipe state::::Those three bytes are removed, leaving the pipe empty with a live writer.~Issue the next read::::The second blocking read waits because neither data nor the all-writers-closed EOF condition exists.','First read returns 3; the next read blocks until data or closure.','The first return cannot be eight because only three bytes existed. An open writer rules out interpreting the empty pipe as EOF.'),
E('trap','A program passes an unmapped user buffer to a syscall that must copy input before updating a kernel object. Can the kernel trust it?','Identify the boundary::::A user pointer is input to the protected service, not permission to access arbitrary memory.~Validate copying::::The required copy discovers the unmapped address and reports the specified bad-address error.~Protect object state::::Because validation precedes the update in this contract, the object remains unchanged.','Reject the request; do not perform the object update.','A correctly mapped buffer would permit the copy. The same numeric argument does not become valid simply because execution entered kernel mode.'))

P('7-processes-process','Processes',r'''
A process is an ownership and execution boundary: it supplies an address space, identifiers, resource references, and a place for schedulable execution. Counting processes requires tracing creation branches, while tracing states requires asking whether execution can make progress using CPU alone. A ready process needs dispatch; a blocked process needs an external event. Exiting removes active execution but may leave a record until the parent collects its status. Fork-style examples assume successful creation and the stated branch conditions; exec replaces an image within the existing process. Draw creation edges separately from state transitions so a program replacement, I/O completion, or wait operation is not accidentally counted as another creation.
''',
E('fundamental','P is running, its quantum expires, it later runs and requests unavailable disk data, and the disk then completes. Trace states.','Expire the quantum::::P becomes ready because it can resume as soon as the scheduler supplies CPU.~Request unavailable data::::After dispatch, the disk wait changes running to blocked; more CPU cannot supply the missing data.~Complete the I/O::::P becomes ready, awaiting a future dispatch rather than automatically becoming the running process.','Running → ready → running → blocked → ready.','Ready states arise from CPU eligibility; the blocked state ends only when the awaited disk event occurs.'),
E('application','One process calls fork. Only the child calls another successful fork. The original parent then successfully execs a new program. No process exits. Count processes.',"First creation::::The original parent and one child now exist.~Conditional creation::::Only that child executes the second fork, producing one additional process; the original does not duplicate again.~Replace the image::::Exec changes the original parent's program without creating a fourth process.",'Three processes, including the original.','There are exactly two successful creation calls, each adding one process; 1+2=3 independently checks the tree.'),
E('trap','A child exits while its parent continues without waiting. Later the parent calls wait and collects the exit status. Was the child blocked awaiting CPU?',"Stop child execution::::Exit ends the child's runnable execution and releases the appropriate active resources.~Retain status::::A zombie record may remain so the parent can obtain its termination status; it is not a ready or blocked worker.~Reap the record::::Wait collects the status and removes the retained termination record.",'The exited child is a zombie until reaped, not a process waiting to run.',"Scheduling the zombie cannot resume its completed program. The parent's wait changes bookkeeping rather than creating another process."))

P('7-processes-threads','Threads',r'''
Threads divide execution state without necessarily dividing ownership of memory. Each needs its own instruction position and saved registers, but threads in one process can address shared objects and, when given valid pointers, objects located on another thread's stack. Storage location does not establish a protection boundary. For performance questions, identify which work can actually run independently before applying a speedup formula; synchronization and serial stages can cap benefits. The examples assume kernel-supported threads, a shared process address space, valid object lifetimes, and enough processors when parallel speedup is requested. Classify ownership, accessibility, lifetime, and synchronization separately, since confusing any two can make a thread design look safer or faster than it is.
''',
E('fundamental','Main creates workers A and B. Each has local counter c; both access one heap queue through a shared pointer. Which state is separate?','Assign execution state::::A and B retain distinct program counters, registers and local counter objects.~Identify shared storage::::Both pointers refer to the same heap queue in the process address space.~Protect the shared invariant::::Queue updates require the specified synchronization, whereas independent c objects do not conflict solely because their names match.','Execution state and local counters are separate; the heap queue is shared.',"Changing A's c does not name B's c, but changing the queue changes the object both workers address."),
E('application','A workload is 90% perfectly parallel and 10% serial. Four worker threads run on four processors with zero extra overhead. Find ideal speedup.','Normalize one-thread time::::Let the original execution take one unit, split into 0.10 serial and 0.90 parallel.~Divide only parallel work::T_4=0.10+0.90/4=0.325::The serial part cannot be assigned simultaneously to all workers.~Compare completion times::S_4=1/0.325=40/13::Speedup is approximately 3.077, below four despite ideal thread execution.','Ideal speedup is 40/13, approximately 3.077.','Four-thread time times speedup equals one. With infinitely many workers the serial fraction still caps speedup at 10.'),
E('trap',"A passes B a pointer to local object v, keeps its function active, and synchronizes B's read after v=9. Is v inaccessible because stacks are separate?","Check addressing::::Both threads share an address space, so B can use a valid pointer to A's stack object.~Check lifetime::::A's function is still active, so the specified object's lifetime has not ended.~Check publication::::The stated synchronization orders initialization before B's read, which therefore observes 9 under this model.",'B can access v and reads 9 under the given lifetime and synchronization assumptions.',"Returning from A's function before the read would change lifetime validity; distinct stacks alone do not supply memory isolation."))

P('7-processes-ipc','Inter-process communication',r'''
An IPC design must specify both its transport and its protocol. A byte stream communicates ordered bytes without promising one receive per send; a bounded channel adds capacity and blocking; endpoint ownership determines when closure becomes visible. Build a small state table containing buffered data, remaining capacity, open endpoints, and each process's next operation. Execute an operation only when its precondition holds. This reveals partial transfers and deadlocks that disappear in an informal story about processes exchanging messages. The examples assume reliable local channels with explicitly stated capacities and blocking behavior. Shared memory, pipes, and message queues have different framing rules, so carry the transport contract through every reasoning step instead of assuming that all IPC preserves application records.
''',
E('fundamental','A byte-stream pipe contains the six bytes ABCDEF. Two reads request four bytes each; all writers close after writing. Trace the returned data.','First request::::Four available bytes satisfy the first request, returning ABCD and leaving EF.~Second request::::Only EF remains, so the next read returns two bytes under the stated available-data behavior.~Observe closure::::After those bytes are consumed, another read returns EOF because no writer remains.','The reads return ABCD, then EF; a subsequent read returns EOF.','The payload lengths sum to six; pipe framing does not create a second four-byte record.'),
E('application','Two processes each have a private incoming channel of capacity two messages. Each sends three messages to the other before receiving any; sends block when full. Determine the state after the first two sends from each.',"Fill both channels::::Each process's first two messages fit, so both incoming channels contain two messages.~Attempt the third sends::::Neither destination has free capacity; both processes block before reaching their receive loops.~Inspect the release condition::::Only a receive can free a slot, but both receivers are blocked sending, producing a protocol deadlock.",'Both processes deadlock on their third sends.','Each channel contains exactly its capacity of two. Alternating a receive before the third send would free space, identifying the missing progress action.'),
E('trap',"A parent and child inherit a pipe's write descriptor. The child closes its writer, the parent keeps its own writer open, and the empty pipe is read in blocking mode. Does child closure guarantee EOF?","Count live writers::::The parent's inherited descriptor still references a live writing endpoint.~Check the empty buffer::::No byte can be returned immediately, but emptiness alone is not closure.~Apply the read contract::::The read blocks because future data can still arrive through the parent's writer.",'No EOF yet; the empty-pipe read blocks while the parent writer remains open.','Closing the remaining parent writer makes the all-writers-closed condition true, after which the same empty read returns EOF.'))

P('7-synchronization-interleavings','Concurrency',r'''
Concurrent reasoning begins with events and dependencies, not a presumed simultaneous execution of whole source statements. Preserve each worker's program order while allowing events from different workers to interleave. Then identify which outcomes follow from the order of reads and writes. An atomic operation reduces the event set but does not make a larger sequence atomic. When enumerating classroom traces, explicitly assume sequentially consistent atomic reads and writes or a simplified read-modify-write model; a language-level data race may have different rules. A single counterexample demonstrates an unsafe invariant, whereas a proposed list of all outcomes needs coverage of every legal ordering. Use read-from relationships to justify values rather than guessing that concurrency always produces an unpredictable number.
''',
E('fundamental','Initially x=0. A executes atomic x=1; B executes atomic r=x. Under sequential consistency, list possible r.',"Read before write::::If B's read occurs first, it reads the initial value 0.~Write before read::::If A's write occurs first, B reads 1.~Bound the possibilities::::Only the initialization and A's write supply values for x; no execution writes another value.",'r can be 0 or 1, and no other value in this model.','Each outcome has a complete legal two-event ordering, and the set of writes independently bounds the result.'),
E('application','x=5. A and B each perform atomic fetch_add(x,1) and store its returned old value in a and b. Find final x and possible (a,b).','Use atomic read-modify-write::::Each fetch_add is one indivisible event that returns its own preceding value.~Order the two events::::A first gives a=5,b=6; B first gives b=5,a=6.~Account for both increments::x_{final}=5+1+1=7::No lost update occurs because both updates are atomic.','Final x=7; returned pairs are (5,6) or (6,5).','The returned old values are distinct consecutive values, and the later event must observe the earlier increment.'),
E('trap',"Under a simplified sequentially consistent model, A reads x=0, B writes x=4, then A stores its saved value plus one. Does the final value include B's update?","Save the old value::::A retains 0 locally before B executes.~Interleave another write::::B changes shared x to 4, without changing A's saved operand.~Complete the stale computation::::A writes 0+1=1 and overwrites the shared value 4.","Final x is 1; A's non-atomic update loses B's write.",'The local read-compute-store order is respected. Making the whole update atomic would exclude this particular interleaving.'))

P('7-synchronization-locks','Synchronization',r'''
Synchronization establishes ordering and visibility as well as exclusion. A worker waiting for a condition needs another operation to make that condition true; therefore it must not retain a resource that prevents the producer from doing so. Predicate-based waiting models the durable state in shared variables, while notifications merely tell a waiter to reconsider that state. Ordering tools such as barriers and semaphores serve different roles from mutex ownership. Choose the primitive by writing the required relation between events, then verify who can satisfy each wait. Assume correctly implemented atomic primitives and the stated memory-order guarantees. The subtle errors usually lie in the protocol around a primitive, such as publishing before initialization or mistaking a wakeup for ownership of an item.
''',
E('fundamental','Worker A initializes data=42 then signals a semaphore initially zero; B waits on that semaphore before reading data. Assume signal/wait publishes preceding writes.',"Initialize the payload::::A completes data=42 before issuing its signal.~Establish the dependency::::B's successful wait requires A's signal because the initial semaphore has no permit.~Read after publication::::The specified synchronization makes A's preceding write visible to B.",'B reads 42 after its wait completes.','Moving the signal before initialization removes the necessary order; the initial zero prevents a successful early read in the given protocol.'),
E('application','Three workers each write one disjoint array element, meet at a barrier, then each sums all three elements. The writes are 2,4,8. What sum is permitted after a publishing barrier?',"Perform independent writes::::Workers update separate elements, so these writes do not compete for one location.~Wait for the group::::No worker passes the barrier until all three have arrived after their writes.~Read the published array::sum=2+4+8=14::The barrier's specified visibility guarantee exposes every completed element.",'Each post-barrier sum is 14.','The barrier waits for three arrivals, not the fastest worker alone. Without that ordering a sum could read an uninitialized element.'),
E('trap','Two consumers wait for count>0. A producer adds one item and broadcasts; C1 removes it before C2 reacquires the mutex. What must C2 do?','Publish one item::::The broadcast wakes contenders but the shared count records only one available item.~Let one consumer win::::C1 obtains the lock and reduces count from one to zero.~Recheck after acquisition::::C2 sees count=0 and waits again in its predicate loop rather than consuming a nonexistent item.','C2 must recheck and wait; a wakeup is not a reserved item.','Only one item was produced, so two successful removals would violate conservation. A while loop handles the changed predicate.'))

P('7-synchronization-critical-section-properties','Critical section',r'''
Protect a critical section by naming the invariant and defining its entry and exit events. Exclusion asks whether two contenders can enter together, progress asks whether a free section can be awarded among willing contenders, and bounded waiting asks whether a particular waiting request can be bypassed indefinitely. These questions can have different answers for the same algorithm. Trace the shared control variables under the stated atomicity assumptions, then test both a contention case and an uncontended case. Strict alternation is an instructive example because it can enforce safety while denying entry to a lone contender. Fair queue-based protocols require every holder eventually to release; no entry algorithm can promise completion if the current owner deliberately never exits.
''',
E('fundamental','A shared account has balance 100. A withdraws 20 and B withdraws 30, each holding the same mutex over read, subtraction and store. Show both possible serial results.','A enters first::::A writes 80 and releases; B then reads 80 and writes 50.~B enters first::::B writes 70 and releases; A then reads 70 and writes 50.~Check the invariant::100-20-30=50::Exclusive entry makes each withdrawal use the preceding completed balance.','Both permitted critical-section orders leave balance 50.','No update can use a pre-lock stale read because the prompt includes the read inside the protected region.'),
E('application','A ticket lock gives requests tickets 7,8,9 in that order; serving=7. Each holder eventually releases by incrementing serving. Bound overtaking for ticket 9.','Identify queue position::::Ticket 9 has exactly tickets 7 and 8 ahead of it.~Advance releases::::Holder 7 releases to serving=8; holder 8 releases to serving=9.~Exclude later arrivals::::Tickets 10 and above cannot enter while serving=9 because equality with their own ticket is required.','Ticket 9 waits for two preceding entries and cannot be overtaken by later tickets.','Serving increases in ticket order, so a later ticket cannot match before 9. Eventual holder release is the stated progress assumption.'),
E('trap','Strict alternation permits A only when turn=A and B only when turn=B. B leaves turn=A, A runs once and sets turn=B, then B stops requesting entry. Can A enter again?',"Complete A's first entry::::A obeys the protocol and sets turn=B on exit.~Inspect the free section::::No process is inside, and A is the only contender, but its condition turn=A is false.~Locate the dependency::::Only inactive B would change turn back, so A cannot make progress despite no contention.",'Mutual exclusion can hold while the progress condition fails.',"The section is empty and B is outside the competition; a protocol that still requires B's action violates the stated progress property."))

P('7-synchronization-semaphores','Semaphore',r'''
A semaphore represents counted permission, so its initial value must match the resource or event being modeled. Successful wait consumes a permit; signal produces or returns one. Blocking waits must be placed before taking locks whose owners need to release or create that permission. For a bounded buffer, distinguish physical free slots, produced items, and temporary reservations made by successful waits. Counts may briefly reflect reservations rather than completed payload operations. The examples use nonnegative available-permit semantics with blocked queues tracked separately. A semaphore is not automatically an ownership-checking mutex, and signaling too often can authorize nonexistent capacity. Trace each successful wait and each matching signal, then check that permissions correspond to real resources or completed work.
''',
E('fundamental','S starts at 2. A waits, B waits, C waits, then A returns its resource and signals. Trace permits and C.',"Consume two permits::::A and B succeed, changing available permits 2→1→0.~Queue the third waiter::::C blocks because zero available permits remain; it does not acquire a third resource.~Return one resource::::A's signal allows C to acquire the returned permit, so B and C now hold the two resources.",'C first blocks, then proceeds after A releases; two resources remain occupied.','At every stage occupied resources plus unassigned permits equals two; blocked C initially owns none.'),
E('application','A bounded buffer has capacity four and two initial items. Producer P performs wait(empty), inserts one item, signal(full). Consumer C then performs wait(full), removes one, signal(empty). Trace final counts.','Initialize the model::empty=2,\\quad full=2::Two slots are unused and two items are ready.~Publish a produced item::::P changes empty to 1, inserts, then full to 3. The buffer now holds three items.~Consume and release::::C changes full to 2, removes one, then empty to 2. Two items and two free slots remain.','Final empty=2, full=2, occupancy=2.','One production and one consumption leave initial occupancy unchanged, independently confirming the count trace.'),
E('trap','An empty one-slot buffer uses full=0 and mutex=1. A consumer locks mutex before wait(full). The producer needs mutex to insert. What happens?','Consumer holds exclusion::::The consumer obtains the only mutex permit.~Wait for nonexistent data::::Its wait(full) blocks because full=0, while retaining mutex.~Prevent the producer::::The producer cannot lock mutex to create and signal an item; neither operation can progress.','The protocol deadlocks; wait(full) must precede taking the buffer mutex.',"The only action that could increase full requires the held mutex. Reversing the consumer's acquisition order removes this dependency."))

P('7-synchronization-locks','Mutex',r'''
A mutex protects an agreed shared invariant, not a particular variable name automatically. Every conflicting access must obey the same protocol, and its protected region must include the read that supplies an update's old value. Locking only a final assignment can serialize stores while preserving stale computations. Ordinary mutexes have ownership: the acquiring execution releases them, unlike a general signaling semaphore. When several objects need locks, a global acquisition order can eliminate circular wait, but does not itself establish fairness or eliminate every long delay. The examples assume correctly implemented exclusive mutexes and ordinary nonrecursive ownership semantics. Inspect the region boundaries, ownership and nesting order before deciding that a short lock call makes the entire surrounding algorithm safe.
''',
E('fundamental','A owns mutex M. B attempts lock(M), A unlocks, then B enters. Trace ownership and blocked state.',"Respect the current owner::::B cannot acquire M while A owns it, so it waits under the stated blocking mutex.~Release ownership::::A's unlock makes M eligible for acquisition; release does not mean two owners exist.~Acquire for the contender::::When B's lock returns successfully, B owns M and may enter its protected region.",'Ownership transfers A → unowned → B; B enters only after acquisition succeeds.',"B's waiting request is not an ownership grant, so no interval contains both A and B as owners."),
E('application','Three locks obey total order L1<L2<L3. T1 needs L1,L3 and T2 needs L2,L3. Give valid acquisition orders and explain why these cannot form a lock-order cycle.','Order each request set::::T1 acquires L1 then L3; T2 acquires L2 then L3.~Orient dependencies::::Every held-to-requested dependency goes from a lower-ranked lock to a higher-ranked lock.~Rule out a cycle::::A directed cycle would need rank to increase around the cycle and return to its start, which is impossible.','Use L1→L3 and L2→L3; the global order prevents circular lock waiting.','A cycle would require at least one descending acquisition such as L3→L1, which the rule excludes.'),
E('trap','x=10. A and B both read x outside M, compute x+1, then lock M only to store the computed result. Is x guaranteed to become 12?','Take stale inputs::::Both reads can observe 10 before either protected store.~Serialize only assignments::::A stores 11 while holding M; B later also stores its saved 11 while holding M.~Inspect invariant coverage::::The read-modify-write update spans outside the lock, so store exclusion does not protect the complete increment.','x can finish at 11 despite mutually exclusive stores.','Both critical-section entries are legal and nonoverlapping. Moving each read and computation inside M excludes the stale-input trace.'))

P('7-deadlock-conditions','Deadlock',r'''
Deadlock is a property of a waiting set and its remaining ways to make progress. Identify held resources, pending requests and the operations that release resources. A cycle proves deadlock in a single-instance, nonpreemptible resource model only when the participants cannot take another permitted action that breaks it. Multiple instances require available-count reasoning rather than a cycle shortcut. Detection uses current requests, whereas avoidance considers possible future maximum claims; an unsafe state need not already be blocked. Assume the stated resource-release rules and no unmentioned timeout or external recovery. Build the wait relationships first, then distinguish an actual closed waiting set from one unlucky process that is merely delayed while others can finish.
''',
E('fundamental','P holds the only printer and requests the only scanner; Q holds the scanner and requests the printer. Neither releases before obtaining both.',"Build P's dependency::::P waits for Q to release the scanner.~Build Q's dependency::::Q waits for P to release the printer.~Check permitted progress::::Neither reaches a release without the other's held resource, and no extra instance exists.",'P and Q are deadlocked.','Both resources are accounted for and both release paths are blocked; the P→Q→P wait cycle is sufficient here.'),
E('application','Resource R has two instances, S one. P holds R and requests S; Q holds S and requests R; one R instance is free. Is the apparent P/Q cycle deadlocked?','Inspect available instances::::Q can receive the second R instance despite P holding the first.~Complete Q::::After obtaining R, Q can finish and release S plus its R instance under the stated completion rule.~Complete P::::P then obtains S and finishes, breaking the apparent cycle.','Not deadlocked; Q,P is a completion sequence.','The free R instance supplies the request that the simple graph cycle ignored. Final availability recovers two R instances and one S.'),
E('trap','One resource has total capacity three. P and Q each hold one, can need two more each, but currently request nothing. Is the unsafe maximum-claim state already deadlocked?','Compute possible future need::::Each remaining claim is two while only one unit is available, so no maximum-demand safe sequence is established.~Inspect current requests::::Neither process is currently blocked requesting a resource.~Separate classifications::::The state can be unsafe without being an existing deadlock; a process could finish without exercising its maximum claim.','Unsafe under the stated maximum claims, but not currently deadlocked.','Deadlock requires actual inability to proceed; the example deliberately has no current waiting request.'))

P('7-deadlock-conditions','Coffman conditions',r'''
The four Coffman conditions describe the standard resource deadlock model: exclusive use, retaining resources while requesting more, inability to forcibly reclaim held resources, and a circular dependency. They are necessary together; merely observing one condition does not prove deadlock. Prevention changes a protocol so at least one condition cannot arise. Avoidance instead admits allocations only when a safety argument survives, and detection allows problematic states then finds them. To classify a prevention policy, name exactly which condition its rule removes and acknowledge its assumptions. Forcibly reclaiming a CPU is straightforward compared with reclaiming a partially written device operation. A policy justified for safely restartable resources need not apply unchanged to every physical resource.
''',
E('fundamental','A task may begin only after atomically obtaining every device it will need. If unavailable, it retains no devices. Which condition is removed?','Inspect waiting tasks::::A failed all-at-once request leaves the task with no allocated device.~Inspect holding tasks::::A successful task already has its full requested set and need not wait for an additional device.~Identify the missing condition::::No task simultaneously holds some devices and waits for another under this protocol.','Hold-and-wait is removed.','Exclusive devices can still exist and preemption need not be allowed; removing this one necessary condition suffices for modeled resource-deadlock prevention.'),
E('application','Three locks have ranks A=1,B=2,C=3. Every task acquires locks in increasing rank. Could tasks holding A, B and C form a circular wait?','Orient every request::::A holder can request only a higher-ranked additional lock.~Follow any wait chain::::Ranks strictly increase along the held-to-requested dependencies.~Try to close the chain::::Returning to the first rank would require a decrease, forbidden by the rule.','The rule removes circular wait.','C cannot be held while requesting A or B, so the final edge needed for a three-lock cycle is absent.'),
E('trap','A system uses exclusive resources, allows holding while waiting, and forbids preemption. Every request graph is nevertheless acyclic. Are three Coffman conditions enough to declare deadlock?','List present conditions::::Mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait and no preemption are present.~Inspect the fourth condition::::The acyclic graph has no circular waiting chain.~Apply necessity::::All four are required together in the stated model, so those three facts do not prove deadlock.','No; the missing circular-wait condition prevents this modeled deadlock.','A terminal holder in an acyclic finite wait chain can progress under the stated completion assumptions, releasing resources upstream.'))

P('7-deadlock-banker','Bankers algorithm',r'''
Banker's algorithm separates present holdings from declared maximum claims. A safety simulation lends remaining needs from a temporary Work vector, imagines completion, and returns the original allocation as the net increase. Componentwise comparison matters: spare printers cannot substitute for a scanner. For a request, verify it does not exceed Need or Available before constructing a tentative state; then repeat the safety test and grant only if every process can finish. An unsafe result means the declared maximum-demand guarantee fails, not that the current CPU execution must already be deadlocked. Assume accurate maximum claims, reusable resource instances and eventual completion once a process's needs can be satisfied. Keep tentative arithmetic separate from committed allocations so a rejected request does not consume real resources.
''',
E('fundamental','Available=(1,1). P allocation=(1,0), need=(0,1); Q allocation=(0,1), need=(2,0). Run the safety test.',"Finish a feasible process::::P's need (0,1) fits Work=(1,1).~Return P's held allocation::Work=(1,1)+(1,0)=(2,1)::Only its original holding is a net addition after hypothetical completion.~Finish Q::Work=(2,1)+(0,1)=(2,2)::Q's need (2,0) now fits, so both can finish.",'Safe sequence P,Q.','Final Work=(2,2) equals Available plus both initial allocation vectors, independently conserving resources.'),
E('application','One resource: Available=2; P allocation=1,max=3; Q allocation=1,max=2. Q requests one. Should Banker grant it?',"Validate the request::::Q need=1 and Available=2, so the request passes both initial bounds.~Construct tentative state::::Available becomes 1; Q allocation becomes 2 and need becomes 0. P still needs 2.~Prove tentative safety::::Q finishes first, making Work=1+2=3; P's need 2 then fits. Grant the request.",'Grant; the tentative safe sequence is Q,P.','Total resources remain four: tentative availability 1 plus allocations 1 and 2. The safety witness does not rely on inventing extra capacity.'),
E('trap','Available=1; P allocation=1,max=3; Q allocation=1,max=2. P requests one. It fits Available and Need. Is that sufficient?','Apply the tentative request::::Available becomes 0; P allocation=2,need=1; Q allocation=1,need=1.~Search for a feasible finish::::Both needs exceed Work=0, so neither can be selected.~Reject without committing::::The tentative state is unsafe; restore the original allocation and availability.',"Deny or postpone P's request despite enough currently available units.",'The original state is safe via Q first, since Q need=1 fits. Granting P destroys that witness and leaves no alternative.'))

P('7-scheduling-cpu','CPU scheduling',r'''
CPU scheduling metrics measure different parts of a process's experience. Response ends at first dispatch, turnaround ends at completion, and waiting counts time eligible but not running. For a single CPU burst with no blocking, waiting can be computed by subtracting service from turnaround; with I/O, subtract blocked time as well. Dispatch policies select only ready work, so arrivals and wakeups change the candidate set. Include priority, tie and switch-cost rules explicitly before drawing intervals. A priority number has no universal interpretation without stating whether smaller or larger is preferred. Build the timeline first and derive per-process metrics from it, rather than inferring latency from utilization or assuming a responsive first dispatch means little later waiting.
''',
E('fundamental','P arrives at 3, first runs at 5, consumes one uninterrupted four-unit burst and completes at 9. Find response, turnaround and waiting.',"Measure first service::R=5-3=2::Response stops at P's first dispatch.~Measure completion::T=9-3=6::Turnaround includes waiting and service.~Remove CPU service::W=6-4=2::No I/O or later preemption exists in this workload.",'Response 2, turnaround 6, waiting 2.','Arrival plus waiting plus service is 3+2+4=9, matching the stated completion.'),
E('application','Preemptive priority scheduling prefers smaller numbers. P arrives 0,burst 6,priority 3; Q arrives 2,burst 2,priority 1. Zero switch cost. Find the timeline and waiting.','Run the initial ready task::::P runs 0–2 because Q has not arrived.~Apply priority at arrival::::Q preempts P at 2 and runs 2–4 to completion; P has four service units left.~Finish and count waiting::::P resumes 4–8. P waits during 2–4 for two units; Q starts immediately and waits zero.','P 0–2, Q 2–4, P 4–8; waiting P=2,Q=0.','P receives 2+4=6 service units and Q receives 2. P turnaround 8 minus service 6 independently gives waiting 2.'),
E('trap','P arrives 0, runs 0–2, blocks for I/O 2–7, waits ready 7–9, and runs 9–10 to finish. Is turnaround minus CPU service its waiting time?','Count service and completion::::CPU service is 3; turnaround is 10.~Separate blocked time::::The interval 2–7 contributes five units unavailable for CPU dispatch, not ready-queue waiting.~Calculate ready waiting::W=10-3-5=2::Only 7–9 is waiting for CPU.','Waiting is 2, not 7.','Intervals partition into CPU 3, blocked 5 and ready waiting 2, totaling turnaround 10.'))

P('7-scheduling-disk','I/O scheduling',r'''
I/O scheduling orders outstanding operations according to the cost model supplied by the question. In a moving-head disk model, seek distance is the absolute change in track number, not elapsed time automatically. FCFS uses request order while SSTF uses nearest currently pending position; directional scans have boundary rules. Each policy can improve one measure while worsening fairness or latency for particular requests. Trace selected requests from the initial head location, updating the pending set after each service. State arrival behavior, direction, endpoint conventions and whether wrap motion counts. These examples use static queues and distance-only costs unless explicitly discussing starvation; applying these mechanical seek assumptions to solid-state storage would require a different model.
''',
E('fundamental','Head=25; queued requests in arrival order are 40,10,35. Use FCFS and count track movement.','Serve the first arrival::|40-25|=15::Move from 25 to 40.~Continue in queue order::|10-40|=30::The nearer later request does not bypass request 10.~Finish the queue::|35-10|=25::Total movement is 15+30+25=70.','FCFS order 40,10,35; movement 70 tracks.','The net displacement is only 10 but the traveled path includes a reversal, so displacement cannot replace distance.'),
E('application','Head=50; static pending tracks are 12,45,57,90. Use SSTF with no ties.','Choose the nearest request::::45 is five away, closer than 57 at seven, so visit 45.~Recompute from the new position::::57 is 12 away versus 12 at 33 and 90 at 45; visit 57.~Complete remaining requests::::90 is 33 away versus 12 at 45, then 12 is 78 away. Total 5+12+33+78=128.','Order 45,57,90,12; movement 128.','Distances are recalculated at each head position. Keeping the original distance order would incorrectly select 12 before 90.'),
E('trap','A distant request at track 900 waits while an unlimited sequence of new requests appears beside the current head near track 10. SSTF always chooses a nearer pending request. Is finite service guaranteed?','Compare eligible distances::::Each new nearby request is closer than the still-pending track 900.~Repeat the selection::::The policy can keep choosing these arrivals, never making the distant request nearest.~Identify the fairness failure::::Other requests progress while one is postponed without bound, which is starvation rather than all requests deadlocking.','SSTF alone does not guarantee service of the distant request.','Stopping new arrivals leaves a finite queue that eventually reaches 900; the counterexample depends on continued competing arrivals.'))

P('7-scheduling-fcfs-arrivals','FCFS',r'''
FCFS orders entries into the ready queue, not a permanent ordering of process identities. A process that blocks leaves that queue and joins again when its event completes. In the ordinary nonpreemptive model, a newly ready process cannot interrupt the current CPU burst, even if it previously arrived before everyone else. The convoy effect illustrates how one long burst delays many short bursts behind it. For calculations, state whether all jobs are initially ready or whether I/O and wakeups occur, and determine tie order when events share a timestamp. Trace queue membership at completion and blocking points before computing averages. Fairness in a finite queue means older queued requests are not bypassed; it does not mean every process experiences the same waiting time.
''',
E('fundamental','All arrive at 0, in queue order A,B,C, with bursts 2,5,1. Compute FCFS starts and mean waiting.','Run the first burst::::A occupies 0–2 and waits zero.~Continue without burst sorting::::B runs 2–7, then C runs 7–8.~Average queue delay::\\bar W=(0+2+7)/3=3::Each start equals waiting because all arrivals are zero.','Starts A=0,B=2,C=7; mean waiting 3.','Total waiting nine equals the sum of the cumulative preceding bursts 0,2,7.'),
E('application','All jobs arrive together. One long job L has burst 9 and three short jobs each burst 1. Compare mean FCFS waiting when L is first versus last.','Put L first::::Waiting times are 0,9,10,11, summing to 30.~Put L last::::Waiting times are 0,1,2,3, summing to 6.~Compare the averages::30/4=7.5,\\quad 6/4=1.5::Arrival/tie order creates a convoy although total service is 12 in both cases.','Mean waiting is 7.5 with L first and 1.5 with L last.','The same four bursts and same total service isolate queue order as the cause of the six-unit mean difference.'),
E('trap','FCFS: A and B arrive 0, A first. A runs 0–1 then blocks until 3; B has burst 4 and starts at 1. A wakes at 3 with one unit remaining. Does A immediately regain CPU?',"Remove blocked A::::At 1, A is unavailable and B begins its nonpreemptive burst.~Rejoin on wakeup::::A becomes ready at 3 behind the currently running burst.~Finish the active burst::::B runs through 5, after which A runs 5–6; A's earlier original arrival does not preempt B.",'A resumes at 5, waiting two units after its wakeup.',"B receives exactly four uninterrupted units 1–5. Ready-queue entry at 3, rather than original process birth, governs A's later queue position."))

P('7-scheduling-sjf-ready-selection','SJF',r'''
SJF compares CPU burst lengths only at dispatch opportunities under its nonpreemptive definition. Known bursts support arithmetic exercises, while real systems may estimate them from past behavior; those estimates are not guarantees about future work. Arrival constraints matter even when every burst is printed in the question. A newly arriving shorter job joins the ready set but waits for the current burst to finish. Repeated short arrivals can indefinitely bypass a long ready job, so minimizing average waiting for a fixed all-ready batch does not prove fairness for an ongoing stream. State the preemption variant and tie rule first. Use separate dispatch-time ready sets to justify every choice rather than presenting only a final sorted list.
''',
E('fundamental','All arrive 0 with distinct bursts A=6,B=2,C=4. Nonpreemptive SJF, zero switching cost. Find order and mean waiting.','Choose the shortest ready burst::::B runs 0–2.~Choose among remaining work::::C runs 2–6, then A runs 6–12.~Compute per-job delays::\\bar W=(0+2+6)/3=8/3::The order here is sorted because all jobs are already available.','Order B,C,A; mean waiting 8/3.','Waiting contributions are the cumulative preceding bursts 0,2,6, and total executed service is 12.'),
E('application','A=(arrival 0,burst 3), B=(1,burst 6), C=(2,burst 2), D=(4,burst 1). Use nonpreemptive SJF with no switch cost.',"Dispatch at zero::::Only A is ready, so A runs 0–3.~Dispatch at three::::B and C are ready; choose C for 3–5. D's arrival at 4 cannot preempt it.~Complete by available length::::D runs 5–6, then B runs 6–12. Waiting A=0,C=1,D=1,B=5.",'Timeline A,C,D,B; mean waiting 7/4 and completions 3,12,5,6 for A,B,C,D.','Every selected burst is shortest in its dispatch-time ready set. Waiting sum seven also follows from completion minus arrival minus service.'),
E('trap','At time 0, long job L of burst 100 and short job S0 of burst 1 are ready. Before each short completion another burst-one job arrives. Nonpreemptive SJF always sees a short job ready. Must L eventually run?',"Choose at the first dispatch::::A burst-one job beats L's burst 100.~Maintain the ready alternative::::A fresh short job exists at each subsequent completion and again beats L.~Classify the outcome::::L remains ready while other jobs complete indefinitely; ordinary SJF permits starvation without an aging rule.",'L has no eventual-service guarantee in this arrival stream.','A finite fixed batch would eventually exhaust the short jobs; the constructed continuing stream is what invalidates the fairness claim.'))

P('7-scheduling-cpu','Round robin',r'''
Round robin converts a ready queue into repeated opportunities to consume at most one quantum. A job finishing early releases the CPU immediately; an unfinished job goes to the tail after its slice under the stated event-order convention. Track remaining service rather than assigning a full quantum even to a one-unit remainder. Switching overhead consumes elapsed time without contributing to any process's required service, so it affects completion and waiting calculations. Specify whether overhead applies before the initial dispatch, after the final completion, and between consecutive slices. With a quantum at least as large as every burst in an initially ready CPU-only batch, the policy behaves like FCFS. Smaller quanta alone do not promise lower total completion time when overhead is material.
''',
E('fundamental','A burst=3 and B burst=1 arrive at 0, A first. Quantum=2, zero overhead. Trace service and waiting.','First slice::::A runs 0–2 and has one unit left, then joins the tail.~Serve the short job::::B runs 2–3 and finishes before using a whole quantum.~Finish A::::A runs 3–4. Waiting A=4−3=1 and B=3−1=2.','A 0–2,B 2–3,A 3–4; waiting A=1,B=2.','CPU intervals total four service units; the short B slice consumes one rather than the full quantum.'),
E('application','A burst=5 and B burst=3 arrive 0, A first. Quantum=2; every change between processes costs one time unit, with no initial or final cost. Find completions.','Execute first slices::::A runs 0–2, switch 2–3, B runs 3–5, switch 5–6.~Execute later slices::::A runs 6–8, switch 8–9, B finishes 9–10, switch 10–11.~Finish and account::::A finishes 11–12. Four switches add four units to eight units of actual CPU service.','Completion A=12,B=10; waiting is 7 for each.','Service 5+3 plus overhead 4 equals makespan 12. Completion minus burst gives 12−5=7 and 10−3=7 because both arrived zero.'),
E('trap','A and B arrive 0, A first, bursts 2 and 3. Round-robin quantum=10, zero overhead. Does the label round robin imply an interleaved execution?',"Check A's remainder::::A finishes in two units, before quantum expiration.~Dispatch B::::B then finishes its three-unit burst without expiration.~Compare FCFS::::The resulting uninterrupted A 0–2,B 2–5 timeline is exactly FCFS for this batch.",'No interleaving occurs; the timeline matches FCFS.','Both bursts fit within one quantum, so there is no unfinished slice to return to the queue.'))

P('7-scheduling-disk','SCAN',r'''
SCAN is directional service with a specified turnaround boundary. Requests along the current direction are served in positional order, then the head continues to the physical endpoint before reversing under the convention used here. LOOK reverses at the last pending request instead, so the extra endpoint trip can change the distance even when request order initially matches. Static-queue calculations end when the final requested track is served unless the question explicitly asks for a complete sweep. Count each required segment, including an endpoint without a request. Initial direction is part of the input. For wrap policies such as C-SCAN, the head does not reverse its service direction, so do not silently use their wrap rule in a SCAN answer.
''',
E('fundamental','Tracks 0…99, head 40, requests 10,30,70. SCAN initially downward, endpoint travel required; stop after last request.','Serve downward requests::::Visit 30 then 10, continuing to endpoint 0.~Reverse direction::::The only remaining request is 70, so travel upward from 0 to 70.~Add the path::(40-0)+(70-0)=110::The movement includes the unrequested endpoint trip.','Service order 30,10,70; total movement 110.','Segment form 10+20+10+70 gives the same 110 and makes the boundary leg visible.'),
E('application','Tracks 0…199, head 120, requests 20,90,150,180. Initially upward; compare SCAN and LOOK movement until final service.','Share the upward service::::Both visit 150 then 180.~Choose the turnaround::::SCAN continues to 199; LOOK reverses at 180.~Finish downward work::D_{SCAN}=(199-120)+(199-20)=258,\\quad D_{LOOK}=(180-120)+(180-20)=220::Both later visit 90 then 20.','SCAN 258, LOOK 220; difference 38 tracks.','The extra trip is twice the 19-track gap from 180 to 199, independently checking the difference.'),
E('trap','Tracks 0…99, head 80, requests 10 and 30, initial SCAN direction upward. May the head reverse immediately because all requests are behind it?','Honor the initial direction::::Under the given boundary-reaching SCAN rule, first travel 80→99.~Reverse at the endpoint::::Then service 30 and 10 in descending order.~Count required movement::(99-80)+(99-10)=108::Immediate reversal would instead be a different convention.','Movement 108; the boundary trip remains required.','The service order alone hides the extra 19-track outward leg and return. A 70-track answer would silently replace the specified SCAN rule.'))

P('7-virtual-files-allocation-fragmentation','Memory management',r'''
Allocation policy answers which available region receives a request, while allocation representation answers whether that request requires a contiguous region at all. First fit selects the first sufficient hole; best fit chooses the smallest sufficient hole, but neither creates space that is absent. Keep the list in address order when simulating a policy, replace a chosen hole by its remainder, and coalesce adjacent free regions only when their physical adjacency is given. Fixed page rounding instead creates unused bytes within allocated units. The examples assume byte capacities rather than hidden allocator headers. Separate payload, allocated capacity and externally free capacity, because adding them indiscriminately hides whether failure comes from insufficient total space or the shape of that space.
''',
E('fundamental','Address-ordered holes are 8,3,6 KiB. Allocate 5 KiB using first fit and, independently, best fit. Show remaining holes.','First-fit selection::::The first 8 KiB hole suffices, leaving 3 KiB in that location.~Best-fit selection::::The 6 KiB hole is the smallest sufficient one, leaving 1 KiB there.~Account for free space::::First fit leaves 3,3,6; best fit leaves 8,3,1. Both retain 12 KiB total.','First fit: [3,3,6]; best fit: [8,3,1] KiB.','Initial free space 17 minus allocated payload 5 equals 12 for either policy.'),
E('application','Allocate independent processes of 3,5,9 KiB using 4 KiB pages. Find total allocated capacity and internal fragmentation.','Round each independently::::Page counts are ceil(3/4)=1, ceil(5/4)=2 and ceil(9/4)=3.~Compute physical capacity::C=(1+2+3)4=24\\ \\text{KiB}::Six whole frames are allocated.~Subtract useful bytes::F=24-(3+5+9)=7\\ \\text{KiB}::Per-process waste is 1,3,3 KiB.','Capacity 24 KiB; internal fragmentation 7 KiB.','Rounding the combined 17 KiB as one process would give five frames and undercount because frames cannot share these independent allocations.'),
E('trap','Contiguous holes of 4 and 7 KiB are separated by an immovable allocation. A request needs 9 KiB; total free is 11 KiB. Can ordinary allocation succeed without relocation?','Compare total capacity::::Eleven free KiB exceed the nine-KiB payload.~Compare the required shape::::The largest contiguous hole is only seven KiB.~Apply the immovability rule::::The intervening allocation cannot be compacted away, so no nine-KiB run exists.','The request fails from external fragmentation.','Combining capacities on paper does not join their physical addresses; a noncontiguous representation would change the assumption.'))

P('7-virtual-files-demand-page-fault','Virtual memory',r'''
Virtual memory provides a mapping contract before it provides residency. A legal address may currently require backing-store retrieval, while an illegal address must not acquire a page simply because memory is available. On a valid nonresident access, distinguish the blocked faulting process from other runnable work while the I/O completes. Fault probabilities are especially important because a millisecond penalty can overwhelm a nanosecond normal path; convert units before weighting. State whether the fault cost replaces the entire access time or is an additional penalty to avoid counting a restarted access twice. These examples assume demand paging, valid mapping checks, and the specified restart behavior, without introducing a particular replacement policy unless one is supplied.
''',
E('fundamental','P accesses a valid nonresident page. A free frame exists and reading the page requires disk I/O. Trace process and mapping states.','Detect nonresidency::::The valid mapping lacks a resident frame, so the access traps for demand paging.~Load into free storage::::Allocate the free frame and block P while the disk reads the page; no victim is needed.~Resume the access::::After I/O, install the resident mapping, make P ready, and restart its instruction when dispatched.','P blocks for the load, then becomes ready with a resident page and restarts after dispatch.','A free frame removes replacement work but does not supply the page contents without loading.'),
E('application','Normal completed access is 100 ns. Page faults occur with probability 0.001 and add exactly 5 ms beyond that normal cost. Compute mean access time.','Convert the extra penalty::5\\ \\text{ms}=5{,}000{,}000\\ \\text{ns}::Use one time unit throughout.~Weight the additional cost::pF=0.001(5{,}000{,}000)=5000\\ \\text{ns}::Only faulting references incur the penalty.~Include the base access::EAT=100+5000=5100\\ \\text{ns}::The prompt defines a penalty in addition to ordinary completion.','Mean access time is 5100 ns, or 5.1 microseconds.','Equivalent complete-path weighting is 0.999×100+0.001×5,000,100=5100 ns.'),
E('trap','A virtual address lies outside every mapping of P. There are many free frames. Should the OS treat the access as a routine demand-page load?','Test address validity::::No mapping authorizes the address for P.~Separate capacity from permission::::Free physical frames do not establish a legal virtual region.~Select the fault outcome::::Report the invalid-access failure under the OS contract rather than install an invented mapping.','No; this is an invalid access, not a valid nonresident page awaiting loading.','A valid mapped but nonresident address would follow demand paging; the missing mapping is the decisive difference.'))

P('7-virtual-files-management','Paging',r'''
Paging treats an address as a page identity plus a position within that page. The page identity selects process-specific metadata; the offset survives translation because pages and frames have equal size. Bit counts are a compact way to perform the same quotient-and-remainder calculation when sizes are powers of two. A linear page table's entry count follows the virtual address range, not the number of presently resident frames. Distinct processes can use the same virtual number with different mappings, and permissions accompany those mappings. The examples assume byte addressing and ordinary fixed-size pages. Keep virtual page number, physical frame number, table size and payload size in separate variables so an attractive address width does not silently become a count of physical allocations.
''',
E('fundamental','A 16-bit virtual address is 0x1234, page size 256 bytes, and its virtual page maps to frame 5. Translate using bit fields.','Choose the fields::256=2^8::The low eight bits are offset and the upper eight are page number.~Extract and map::::Virtual page is 0x12 and offset is 0x34; the table supplies frame 5.~Recombine::PA=5(256)+52=1332=0x0534::Only the page/frame field changes.','Physical address 0x0534, or 1332.','Both addresses have the same low byte 0x34, independently checking preservation of the offset.'),
E('application','A byte-addressed virtual space is 22 bits, pages 1024 bytes, and a single-level entry is 4 bytes. Find the full linear page-table size.','Count offset bits::1024=2^{10}::Ten virtual bits select bytes within a page.~Count virtual pages::N=2^{22-10}=4096::Every possible page needs one entry in a full linear table.~Allocate metadata::S=4096(4)=16384\\ \\text{bytes}::This equals 16 KiB.','The full table occupies 16 KiB.','4096 pages times 1024 bytes reconstructs the 4 MiB virtual range; multiplying by entry width separately yields metadata.'),
E('trap','Processes P and Q access virtual address 0x0123 using 256-byte pages. P maps page 1 to frame 9; Q maps it to frame 4. Must their physical addresses match?','Extract the common virtual fields::::Both use page 1 and offset 0x23=35.~Use process-specific tables::::P selects frame 9 and Q selects frame 4.~Compute different destinations::PA_P=9(256)+35=2339,\\quad PA_Q=4(256)+35=1059::The same virtual number can name different physical storage.','No; P accesses 2339 and Q accesses 1059.','The difference is five frames, 1280 bytes, while both translated addresses retain remainder 35.'))

P('7-virtual-files-vm','TLB',r'''
A TLB is a cache of translation information, including mapping identity and normally access permissions. Its hit rate describes avoiding translation lookup work, not a data-cache hit rate. Construct complete hit and miss paths before averaging, and identify which page-table levels are resident or cached. A miss may consult multiple levels yet still find a resident page; a hit can still reveal a permission failure. Context identity or correctly managed invalidation prevents one process's translations from being used blindly for another. The timing examples assume serial TLB lookup and memory accesses without overlap. Other hardware pipelines require a different equation, so the number of table levels and the meaning of each cost must remain explicit.
''',
E('fundamental','TLB lookup takes 4 ns; a resident one-level page table and data each require 60 ns. Compute complete hit and miss costs.','Follow a translation hit::T_h=4+60=64\\ \\text{ns}::The data access follows the cached translation.~Follow a resident miss::T_m=4+60+60=124\\ \\text{ns}::Read one table entry, then access data.~Identify extra work::::The miss adds one 60 ns memory read, with no disk page fault in this scenario.','Hit 64 ns; resident miss 124 ns.','The difference equals exactly one table read, matching the one-level model.'),
E('application','TLB hit rate is 0.8, lookup 5 ns, memory 80 ns. A miss traverses two uncached resident page-table levels before data; no overlap or faults. Find EAT.','Build the hit path::T_h=5+80=85\\ \\text{ns}::Only one data access follows the lookup.~Build the miss path::T_m=5+3(80)=245\\ \\text{ns}::Two metadata accesses precede the data access.~Weight complete alternatives::EAT=0.8(85)+0.2(245)=117\\ \\text{ns}::Hit and miss probabilities sum to one.','EAT is 117 ns.','Base hit cost 85 plus miss probability 0.2 times extra 160 also equals 117, independently checking the result.'),
E('trap','A TLB hit supplies a resident page mapping marked read-only. The instruction attempts a write. Does a hit guarantee successful memory access?','Use the cached mapping::::The translation is found without a page-table walk.~Check the access type::::The requested write conflicts with the cached read-only permission.~Reject the operation::::Raise the specified protection failure rather than write merely because translation hit.','The write fails permission checking despite a TLB hit.','A read could use this same mapping successfully; translation presence and authorization are separate checks.'))

P('7-virtual-files-replacement','Page replacement',r'''
A replacement trace combines a resident set with policy-specific history. FIFO needs load order, LRU needs last-reference order, and optimal replacement needs future references. Hits affect those histories differently, so record the policy state after each reference rather than only listing page contents. Empty-frame loads count as faults although no eviction occurs. Dirty victim status influences I/O cost, not the definition of a page fault. The stack property of LRU and optimal prevents their usual fault counts worsening when more frames are supplied, but FIFO lacks that guarantee. These examples assume all referenced pages are valid, initially empty frames unless specified, and no speculative prefetch. Number hits and faults explicitly to make both the count and the chosen victim auditable.
''',
E('fundamental','Three empty frames, FIFO, references 1,2,3,1,4. Show fault count and victim.','Fill the frames::::References 1,2,3 fault and load in that order.~Process the hit::::The second reference to 1 hits and leaves FIFO load order 1,2,3 unchanged.~Load the missing page::::Reference 4 faults and evicts oldest-loaded 1, yielding resident 2,3,4.','Four faults; victim 1; final residents {2,3,4}.','Five references contain one hit, so faults=5−1=4 independently checks the count.'),
E('application','Two empty frames, optimal replacement, references 1,2,3,2,1. Show the decision at reference 3 and total faults.','Load initial pages::::1 and 2 each fault, filling both frames.~Inspect future uses::::When 3 arrives, 2 is needed next while 1 is needed later; optimal evicts 1.~Finish the string::::2 hits; final 1 faults and may evict either page never used again. Four references fault overall.','Evict 1 at reference 3; total faults 4.','Only the fourth reference is a hit. Choosing 2 at the third reference would force an avoidable fourth-reference fault.'),
E('trap','For FIFO on references 1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5, compare three versus four initially empty frames.','Trace three frames::::Faults occur on positions 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,10,11; hits occur at 8,9,12.~Trace four frames::::Faults occur at 1,2,3,4,7,8,9,10,11,12; positions 5 and 6 hit.~Compare counts::F_3=9,\\quad F_4=10::Additional frames increase faults under FIFO on this string.','FIFO exhibits Belady anomaly: nine faults with three frames, ten with four.',"Listed fault positions independently count nine and ten; FIFO's queue updates on faults, not on hits."))

P('7-files-path-metadata-lookup','File systems',r'''
File-system reasoning has several layers: directories resolve names, object metadata identifies attributes and storage, and allocation maps locate payload bytes. Hard links can give one object several directory names, while open handles can keep an unlinked object accessible until their references are released. Disk I/O counting additionally depends on whether directory, inode and data pages are cached or share a physical block. Write the exact path-resolution chain and distinguish object identifiers from block addresses. These examples assume inode-style object identity, specified POSIX-like link/open lifetime semantics, and ordinary caching rather than hidden automatic prefetch. A naming operation can change reachability without changing data contents, so deleting one name must not automatically be equated with destroying every existing reference to the object.
''',
E('fundamental','Cached directory / maps file notes to inode 18. Its uncached inode occupies one block and names uncached data block 70. Read the first byte; count reads.',"Resolve the name::::The cached directory supplies inode 18 without a disk read.~Load the object's metadata::::Read the inode's one metadata block to obtain data address 70.~Read the payload::::Read block 70, for two disk reads in total.",'Two reads: inode metadata and data.','Inode number 18 is an identifier, not the payload block number; directory caching removes only the name-lookup read.'),
E('application','One inode has hard-link names a and b, link count 2, and one open descriptor. Unlink a, unlink b, then close the descriptor. Trace lifetime.','Remove the first name::::Unlink a reduces link count to 1; b still resolves the same inode.~Remove the last name::::Unlink b reduces link count to 0, but the open descriptor preserves access to the object.~Release the final reference::::Closing that descriptor permits reclamation under the stated link/open semantics.','The object survives both unlinks while open, then becomes reclaimable at close.','Naming references fall 2→1→0 while the open reference remains one until the last step.'),
E('trap','/x and /y are hard links to the same inode. A writes through /x. Does /y retain an independent older payload?',"Resolve both names::::Both entries identify the same object, not separate file copies.~Update shared object storage::::The write changes that inode's payload.~Read through the alias::::A later properly ordered read through /y observes the changed object contents.",'No; both names access the same payload.','Two names increase link count, not the number of independent inodes; copying a file would be a different operation.'))

P('7-files-contiguous-byte-lookup','Contiguous allocation',r'''
A contiguous file extent makes position lookup arithmetic because all allocated blocks follow the starting block in order. The metadata must still supply the logical size or allocated length to reject an out-of-range request. Space after the extent governs simple growth; free space somewhere else cannot be appended without relocating data or adding extents. The representation therefore trades a compact map and good locality for placement constraints. The examples assume one extent, fixed-size blocks and zero-based logical block indices. Count user bytes separately from rounded allocated bytes, and state whether relocated copies coexist temporarily. A direct computed address is valid only after a bounds check, so the simplicity of start-plus-index does not authorize every numerically adjacent block.
''',
E('fundamental','A four-block file starts at physical block 200, block size 512 bytes. Locate logical block 3 and byte offset 1700.','Map the logical block::200+3=203::The fourth file block is physical block 203.~Split the byte request::1700=3(512)+164::The requested byte lies within logical block 3.~Combine location::PA=203(512)+164=104100::Preserve the within-block offset.','Logical block 3 is physical 203; byte 1700 maps to physical byte 104,100.','104,100=200×512+1700, the direct extent formula independently checking the quotient method.'),
E('application','A 2500-byte file uses 1024-byte blocks in one contiguous extent. Determine allocated blocks, internal slack, and the extra adjacent blocks needed to grow to 4500 bytes.','Round the initial size::N_1=\\lceil2500/1024\\rceil=3::Capacity is 3072 bytes.~Compute initial slack::3072-2500=572::These bytes lie inside the allocated final block.~Round the new size::N_2=\\lceil4500/1024\\rceil=5::Growth requires two consecutive free blocks immediately after the existing extent.','Three initial blocks, 572 bytes slack, and two adjacent extra blocks for growth.','Five blocks provide 5120 bytes; four provide 4096, insufficient for 4500.'),
E('trap','A file extent starts at block 30 and has length 3. A request asks for logical block 3. Is start+3=33 a valid lookup?','Enumerate the valid range::::Length three permits zero-based logical indices 0,1,2.~Check the requested index::::Index 3 is outside the extent despite the arithmetic being defined.~Protect the neighboring block::::Block 33 is not allocated to this file by the supplied map, so reject the request.','No; logical block 3 is out of range.','The extent comprises physical blocks 30,31,32; a fourth block would require allocation and a length update.'))

P('7-files-allocation','Linked allocation',r'''
Linked allocation places successor addresses in a chain, allowing physical blocks to be scattered while preserving file order. Metadata names the first block; reaching a later logical block requires following earlier links unless an auxiliary map is supplied. A link stored inside a block also reduces payload capacity, so file-size arithmetic must use usable bytes rather than full physical bytes. Growth can append a block from another location without finding an adjacent run, but a damaged link can disconnect the remaining suffix. The examples assume pointers embedded in data blocks and uncached traversal. A FAT-style table would move the chain metadata elsewhere and change I/O behavior, so do not silently import cached-table advantages into the embedded-pointer model.
''',
E('fundamental','Metadata gives a linked chain of physical blocks 8→41→6→90→EOF. All blocks are uncached. Locate logical block 2 and count reads through its payload.','Read the first block::::Physical 8 supplies the successor 41.~Read the second block::::Physical 41 supplies successor 6.~Read the target::::Physical 6 is logical block 2; three block reads include its payload.','Logical block 2 is physical 6; three reads from the chain start.','The physical numbers are not sorted and two pointer traversals precede the target read.'),
E('application','Physical blocks are 512 bytes and each reserves a four-byte successor pointer. Store a 1400-byte file using embedded linked allocation.','Find usable payload::U=512-4=508\\ \\text{bytes}::The successor occupies storage in every allocated block.~Round required blocks::N=\\lceil1400/508\\rceil=3::Two payload blocks hold only 1016 bytes.~Account for storage::::Three blocks use 1536 physical bytes, including 12 pointer bytes and 124 unused payload bytes.','Three blocks; 12 bytes of pointers and 124 bytes of unused payload capacity.','1400+12+124=1536 independently partitions the physical allocation.'),
E('trap','A four-block embedded chain is 7→12→40→55. The link stored in block 12 is corrupted and becomes EOF. Are blocks 40 and 55 still reachable through the normal file chain?','Follow valid initial links::::The traversal reaches 7 then 12.~Read the corrupted successor::::EOF terminates the chain at logical block 1.~Inspect suffix reachability::::No remaining chain edge names 40, so neither 40 nor its successor 55 is reachable through this metadata.','The suffix 40,55 becomes unreachable from the file start.','Those blocks can still physically exist; loss of reachability differs from deletion of their bytes.'))

P('7-files-indexed-byte-lookup','Indexed allocation',r'''
An indexed file separates the map from its payload. This allows a logical block number to choose one stored address without visiting all earlier data, but metadata capacity and cache residency still determine cost. A single index block has a finite pointer count; reserved bytes reduce that count before any rounding. Multi-level layouts add addressable regions with different read paths. The examples assume explicit single-level indexing unless stated otherwise, and file metadata is already available when counting index and data reads. Index blocks consume physical storage but are not extra user payload blocks. Check both the logical range and the chosen index slot; an arbitrary pointer value provides location, not proof that the requested block belongs to a valid file position.
''',
E('fundamental','An uncached index has entries [11,80,4]. Cached metadata names it. Read logical block 1; the corresponding data is also uncached.','Select the slot::::Zero-based logical index 1 chooses the second pointer.~Read metadata::::Fetch the index block and obtain physical address 80.~Read payload::::Fetch data block 80; the operation uses two reads without accessing data blocks 11 or 4.','Physical block 80; two reads.','The number of preceding logical blocks does not add payload-chain traversal in indexed allocation.'),
E('application','An index block is 2048 bytes with 16 bytes reserved and eight-byte pointers. Data blocks are 2048 bytes. Find one-index maximum payload and storage when full.','Remove index overhead::2048-16=2032\\ \\text{bytes}::Only this region can hold addresses.~Count targets::k=2032/8=254::The single index addresses 254 data blocks.~Separate capacities::payload=254(2048)=520192,\\quad storage=255(2048)=522240::Physical storage additionally includes the index block.','Maximum payload 520,192 bytes; full allocation 522,240 bytes.','Storage minus payload equals exactly one 2048-byte index block.'),
E('trap','An inode and a single index block are cached; target payload is uncached. A student counts two disk reads because indexed allocation always has an index and data. Correct the count.','Identify required objects::::The lookup uses the index logically, then the target data.~Apply cache residency::::The index access is satisfied from memory and causes no disk read.~Fetch only missing storage::::Read the one uncached data block.','One disk read, although two logical objects are consulted.','Evicting the index would restore the extra metadata disk read; allocation representation alone does not fix cache cost.'))

P('7-files-inodes','Inodes',r'''
An inode is object metadata, not a filename and not merely one data pointer. It can store direct addresses plus several levels of indirect addressing, permissions, size and link information. Determine which addressing region contains a logical block before counting I/O. With k pointers per index block, a double-indirect region has k groups of k data pointers, so its region-relative index splits by quotient and remainder. Object metadata caching removes the inode read but not automatically the indirect blocks. The examples assume a simplified explicit pointer layout and no shared metadata pages. Hard-link names refer to the same inode; symbolic links instead contain a path-like target under different rules. These distinctions prevent file-capacity and name-count calculations from being mixed together.
''',
E('fundamental','Directory names red and blue both contain inode number 42. The inode has link count 2 and one direct data pointer. How many file objects and payload copies are implied?','Resolve identity::::Both names select the same inode 42.~Interpret link count::::Two counts represent directory references to that single object.~Interpret allocation::::The one direct pointer describes one shared payload mapping, not one copy per name.','One file object with two names and one shared payload mapping.','If two independent files existed, the directory entries would identify two different inode objects.'),
E('application','An inode has two direct pointers, one single-indirect and one double-indirect pointer. Index blocks hold four pointers. Locate logical block 11 and count uncached index/data reads with inode cached.','Find its addressing region::::Direct blocks are 0–1; single-indirect blocks 2–5; double-indirect starts at 6.~Split the region index::j=11-6=5=1(4)+1::Use double-root entry 1 and then child-index entry 1.~Follow the read path::::Read the double-indirect root, selected child index, and data block: three disk reads.','Double-indirect root slot 1, child slot 1; three reads.','The selected group covers logical blocks 10–13, and slot 1 is 11; the cached inode adds no fourth read.'),
E('trap','An inode has three direct pointers and one single-indirect pointer; each 1024-byte index holds 256 addresses. Is payload capacity 260 blocks because the indirect index also occupies one block?','Count direct payload::::Three direct targets hold user data.~Count indirect payload::::The indirect index points to 256 additional payload blocks.~Separate metadata::::The index itself stores addresses, giving 259 payload blocks and 260 allocated blocks when full.','Payload capacity is 259 KiB, while full data-plus-index allocation is 260 KiB.','Three plus 256 equals 259; adding the index to storage is correct only after labeling it metadata.'))

P('8-er-relations-er','ER-model',r'''
ER modeling captures identity and relationship rules before tables are chosen. Ask whether an attribute belongs to an entity, to one participation, or to a repeated relationship event. Maximum cardinality and mandatory participation answer different questions: allowing only one advisor does not require every student to have one. Weak entities borrow an owner's identity and add a discriminator that is unique only within that owner. Translate the rules into keys, foreign keys, uniqueness and nullability after clarifying repetition assumptions. The examples use ordinary entity/relationship notation without relying on a particular arrow convention. Test the resulting schema using two concrete objects, because an apparently sensible identifier often fails as soon as two owners reuse the same local label.
''',
E('fundamental','Each employee belongs to exactly one department; a department can have many employees. Design the relationship mapping.','Identify independent entities::::Department(dept_id) and Employee(emp_id) each need their own identifying key.~Place the many-side reference::::Employee stores dept_id referencing Department, since each employee has one department.~Encode required participation::::Declare Employee.dept_id NOT NULL; do not make it UNIQUE because many employees may share a department.','Use a nonnull department foreign key in Employee without child-side uniqueness.','Two employees with different emp_id values and the same dept_id satisfy the described many-to-one relationship.'),
E('application','Order lines use line_no unique only within an order. Order 100 and 200 both have line 1. Model line identity and ownership.','Locate the discriminator::::line_no=1 alone cannot distinguish the two supplied lines.~Include owner identity::::OrderLine(order_id,line_no,item) uses composite key (order_id,line_no).~Enforce the owner::::order_id references Order; each line requires a matching owner under the weak-entity model.','The line key is (order_id,line_no), with a mandatory Order reference.','Pairs (100,1) and (200,1) are distinct keys although their local discriminator matches.'),
E('trap','Each student has at most one advisor, and some students have none. Must the Advisor relationship have total Student participation?','Read the maximum rule::::At most one limits the number of advisors for a participating student.~Read the minimum rule::::Some students have none, giving minimum participation zero.~Choose the mapping::::A nullable advisor_id foreign key can represent absence; NOT NULL would impose an unstated requirement.','Student participation is optional, not total.','A student with advisor_id NULL is explicitly legal under the scenario, refuting the total-participation claim.'))

P('8-er-relations-relation-set-semantics','Relational model',r'''
The relation abstraction concerns values and constraints rather than the storage order of rows. Two instances containing the same full tuples are the same relation even if printed in a different order. Repeated values in one column do not collapse tuples whose other values differ, whereas repeating a complete tuple has no extra multiplicity in the mathematical model. Projection can make originally different tuples equal and therefore removes the resulting duplicates. SQL bags deliberately retain multiplicity unless requested otherwise. These examples assume named attributes and set-based relational operations; distinguish this from presentation sorting and implementation row identifiers. Calculate results by writing full output tuples, then applying the operation's duplicate rules, rather than counting distinct values prematurely.
''',
E('fundamental','A printed relation R(A,B) lists (1,p),(1,p),(2,p),(2,q). How many mathematical tuples and distinct A values does it contain?','Remove repeated full tuples::::The second (1,p) repeats an existing member of the set.~Retain different combinations::::(2,p) and (2,q) remain separate because their B values differ.~Count the two objects::::R contains three tuples, while projection onto A contains values 1 and 2.','Three tuples and two distinct A values.','The surviving full set {(1,p),(2,p),(2,q)} explicitly contains three members.'),
E('application','R(A,B)={(1,x),(2,x)} and S(A,B)={(2,x),(3,y)}. Compare projection onto B of their set union with SQL UNION ALL followed by ordinary SELECT B.','Form the set union::::R union S has three full tuples because (2,x) appears once.~Project set values::::B values x,x,y collapse to {(x),(y)}, giving two tuples.~Keep bag multiplicity::::UNION ALL retains four source rows, and ordinary SELECT B returns x,x,x,y.','Set projection has two tuples; the SQL bag result has four rows.','Source counts 2+2=4 explain the bag cardinality, while overlapping full tuple and projected x account for the set reductions.'),
E('trap','R is printed as (a,1),(b,2); S is printed as (b,2),(a,1) with the same schema. Are these different relations because row order differs?','Compare schemas::::Both use the same named attributes and domains.~Compare tuple membership::::Each full tuple in R also belongs to S, and conversely.~Ignore display order::::A mathematical relation is a set, so printing order is not part of its identity.','R and S are the same relation.','Both set differences R−S and S−R are empty, independently establishing equality.'))

P('8-er-relations-foreign-key-actions','Integrity constraints',r'''
Constraint reasoning checks each rule against the attempted state, not against an application's intentions. A foreign key constrains the referenced values but does not generally prevent many child rows referring to one parent. Composite keys require a whole matching tuple; separate matches for each component can be insufficient. SQL check constraints and null handling also need their exact contract: a nullable value can make a predicate UNKNOWN, which ordinary CHECK accepts unless another constraint forbids null. The examples assume enforced constraints and ordinary SQL three-valued logic. Separate uniqueness, nullability, allowed domains and referential matching in the trace, then identify the particular failed rule. A rejection by one constraint does not mean every other aspect of the proposed row was invalid.
''',
E('fundamental','Parent(id PRIMARY KEY) contains 5. Child(child_id PRIMARY KEY,parent_id REFERENCES Parent) receives (1,5),(2,5),(3,5). Is this legal?','Check child identifiers::::The child keys 1,2,3 are distinct.~Check references::::Every parent_id=5 matches the existing parent key.~Avoid inventing uniqueness::::The foreign key imposes no UNIQUE rule on parent_id, so repeated references are permitted.','All three child rows are legal.','One parent and three distinct children is the standard many-to-one relationship a foreign key can represent.'),
E('application','Parent has composite keys (1,A) and (2,B). Child has a nonnull composite foreign key (number,letter). Test proposed child value (1,B).','Check the entire tuple::::The foreign key requests parent key (1,B), not two independent component matches.~Enumerate parent keys::::Neither (1,A) nor (2,B) equals the requested pair.~Reject the mismatch::::The proposed child violates referential integrity even though 1 and B each occur somewhere.','Reject (1,B): no matching composite parent key exists.','Matching (1,A) would succeed; componentwise membership cannot replace tuple equality.'),
E('trap','A nullable score column has CHECK(score>=0 AND score<=100). Insert score=NULL under ordinary SQL CHECK semantics. Does CHECK alone reject it?','Evaluate comparisons::::Both comparisons with NULL are UNKNOWN.~Combine the predicate::::UNKNOWN AND UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN, not FALSE.~Apply CHECK semantics::::A CHECK rejects FALSE; UNKNOWN passes, so only an additional NOT NULL rule would forbid this value.','The nullable NULL value passes this CHECK.','A score of 101 produces FALSE and is rejected, illustrating the different treatment of FALSE and UNKNOWN.'))

P('8-er-relations-relations','Keys',r'''
A key proof needs determination and minimality over the schema constraints. Compute closure to prove determination, then remove attributes to check that no proper subset still determines everything. Attributes never appearing on any dependency right side must occur in every key unless a special empty determinant is supplied. Candidate keys may overlap and need not coincide with the chosen primary key; prime status is defined using all of them. The examples assume ordinary functional dependencies on a finite attribute set and no unlisted dependencies. Use necessary attributes to prune candidate search, but still prove sufficiency and minimality. An identifier unique in a sample gives evidence about that sample, not a guarantee for every legal database state.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A,B,C) has A→B and B→C. Classify A, AB and B as candidate key, superkey or nonkey.','Compute determining closure::A^+=ABC::A determines the complete schema and is minimal as a singleton.~Check the enlarged set::::AB also determines ABC but contains removable B, so it is a nonminimal superkey.~Check the other singleton::B^+=BC::B cannot determine A and is not a superkey.','A is a candidate key; AB is a nonminimal superkey; B is not a key.','No dependency derives A, so every key must contain A, excluding B independently.'),
E('application','R(A,B,C,D,E) has AB→C,C→A,D→E. Find all candidate keys.','Find necessary attributes::::B and D never occur on right sides, so every key includes BD.~Supply the missing determinant::::Adding A gives ABD, while adding C gives BCD. Either closure reaches all five attributes.~Check minimality and completeness::::BD alone cannot derive A or C; no other attribute supplies them. Thus ABD and BCD are the only minimal possibilities.','Candidate keys ABD and BCD.','Removing B or D loses an underivable attribute; removing A from ABD or C from BCD leaves insufficient BD.'),
E('trap','R(A,B,C) has candidate keys A and BC. A is selected as primary key. Are B and C nonprime because they are absent from the primary key?','Use the definition::::Prime means membership in any candidate key.~Inspect both candidates::::A belongs to A, while B and C belong to BC.~Separate selection from property::::Choosing A for implementation does not erase the alternate candidate key.','All three attributes are prime.','The candidate-key list itself supplies a membership witness for every attribute.'))

P('8-queries-algebra','Relational algebra',r'''
Relational algebra supplies compositional operations with explicit input and output schemas. Selection keeps columns while filtering rows; projection changes the schema and applies set duplicate elimination. Set difference requires compatible relations, and division encodes a for-every association rather than an ordinary one-match join. Before evaluating an expression, track which attributes remain available to later predicates. A plausible-looking operator order can be undefined if it drops a required column. These examples assume mathematical set semantics and attribute names rather than SQL bags. Derive intermediate relations explicitly, especially in division, where a candidate missing even one required partner must be removed. Empty divisor behavior follows the chosen candidate universe, so state where candidate identifiers come from.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A)={1,2,4}, S(A)={2,3}. Evaluate R−S and R intersect S.','Test left membership::::Values 1,2,4 are candidates for difference.~Remove shared members::::2 occurs in S; 1 and 4 do not, so R−S={1,4}.~Keep shared members::::Intersection retains only 2, giving {2}.','Difference {1,4}; intersection {2}.','The two disjoint results partition R: {1,4} union {2}={1,2,4}.'),
E('application','Enrollment(student,course)={(Ann,OS),(Ann,DB),(Bob,OS),(Cam,DB)} and Required(course)={OS,DB}. Evaluate Enrollment divided by Required.','List candidate students::::Ann, Bob and Cam each appear in Enrollment.~Search missing requirements::::Ann has both; Bob lacks DB; Cam lacks OS.~Retain universal matches::::Only Ann has a pair with every Required course.','The division result is {Ann}.',"Ann's required set difference is empty, while Bob and Cam each have an explicit missing-course witness."),
E('trap','R(A,B) is projected to A, then a selection B=7 is applied. Is sigma(B=7)(pi(A)(R)) a valid ordinary algebra expression?','Track the output schema::::Projection onto A removes B.~Inspect predicate requirements::::Selection B=7 needs a B attribute in its input.~Reject the expression::::The required attribute is unavailable; apply selection before projection if that is the intended query.','The expression is undefined under the stated schema.','pi(A)(sigma(B=7)(R)) is well-formed because the predicate is evaluated while B still exists.'))

P('8-queries-calculus','Tuple calculus',r'''
Tuple calculus expresses predicates over candidate tuples rather than an execution plan. The output variable must be tied to a finite relation or otherwise safely bounded; existential variables provide matching witnesses, and universal requirements can be stated as the absence of a missing witness. Scope controls whether one witness must satisfy all candidates or separate witnesses may exist for different candidates. These examples use relation membership and ordinary logical quantifiers, with student identifiers drawn from Student. Check a query by testing each candidate and exhibiting either its witness or a counterexample. Negation alone does not provide a finite domain. An unrestricted universe of possible tuples can make a superficially simple negative condition return infinitely many values unrelated to the database.
''',
E('fundamental','Student IDs are {1,2,3}; Enrollment has (1,OS),(2,DB). Evaluate bounded students for which there exists an Enrollment with matching ID and course OS.','Bound output candidates::::Only IDs in Student can be returned.~Find existential witnesses::::Student 1 has witness (1,OS); student 2 has only DB; student 3 has none.~Emit satisfying candidates::::Return 1 because its matching OS enrollment makes the predicate true.','Result {1}.','The explicit witness proves inclusion of 1; absence of an OS witness proves exclusion of 2 and 3.'),
E('application','Student={Ana,Ben,Cid}; Required={OS,DB}; Enrollment={(Ana,OS),(Ana,DB),(Ben,OS)}. Evaluate students with no required course lacking an enrollment.','Choose bounded students::::Ana, Ben and Cid are the only candidates.~Build counterexamples::::Ben lacks DB; Cid lacks both. Ana has no missing required course.~Negate missing existence::::Only Ana satisfies NOT EXISTS(required course with no matching enrollment).','Result {Ana}.','The counterexample formulation is equivalent to requiring an enrollment for every member of Required.'),
E('trap','Student is finite. The expression {t | NOT Student(t)} ranges over an unlimited tuple universe without a positive bound. Is its result necessarily a finite database query?','Inspect variable range::::Nothing restricts t to existing database values.~Apply negation::::Every tuple outside finite Student satisfies the condition.~Evaluate safety::::An unlimited universe contains indefinitely many such tuples, so the expression is unsafe/domain-dependent.','No; unrestricted negation does not guarantee a finite, safe result.','Bounding t with another finite relation would change the query and make its candidate set finite.'))

P('8-queries-sql','SQL',r'''
SQL evaluation uses rows, nulls and three-valued predicates, so a direct translation from ordinary two-valued arithmetic can be wrong. WHERE retains TRUE only; UNKNOWN is discarded like FALSE for filtering but behaves differently under negation and constraints. Correlated subqueries bind inner comparisons to the current outer row, and ties can yield multiple maxima unless uniqueness is guaranteed. Anti-joins using NOT IN require particular care when the subquery includes NULL. These examples assume ordinary SQL semantics with no implicit ordering or duplicate removal. Enumerate the actual rows and predicate truth values before counting output. A returned set's cardinality and an aggregate's denominator depend on which operation ignores nulls, rather than one global rule that every null is zero or always disappears.
''',
E('fundamental','T contains values 2,NULL,5. Compare WHERE value>3 with WHERE value IS NULL.','Evaluate numeric predicates::::2>3 is FALSE, NULL>3 is UNKNOWN, and 5>3 is TRUE.~Filter TRUE rows::::The numeric filter returns only value 5.~Evaluate null testing::::IS NULL directly recognizes the null row and returns it alone.','value>3 returns {5}; value IS NULL returns the one NULL row.','Neither treating NULL as zero nor writing value=NULL reproduces the direct IS NULL predicate.'),
E('application','Scores has (A,10),(A,20),(A,20),(B,7). Return rows whose score equals a correlated subquery MAX(score) for their own department.',"Bind department groups::::For A the inner maximum is 20; for B it is 7.~Test each outer row::::Both separate A rows with 20 equal their department maximum; A's 10 does not.~Retain bag rows::::The result includes (A,20) twice and (B,7) once without DISTINCT.",'Three output rows: two (A,20) and one (B,7).','A maximum value does not select a unique row; duplicate/tied source rows remain unless the query removes them.'),
E('trap','Candidates are 1 and 3; a NOT IN subquery returns 2 and NULL. Evaluate candidate 3 and the complete WHERE result.','Compare candidate three::::3 differs from 2, but comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN.~Negate membership::::The unresolved NULL comparison makes 3 NOT IN (2,NULL) UNKNOWN.~Filter the candidates::::1 has the same issue, so neither predicate is TRUE and the result is empty.','Candidate 3 is not returned; the complete result is empty.','A correlated NOT EXISTS checking equality to candidate IDs would return both 1 and 3 because the null matches neither.'))

P('8-queries-joins','Joins',r'''
A join chooses pairs according to a relationship, then applies its preservation and multiplicity rules. A self join uses aliases to express two roles from the same source without conflating them. Outer joins distinguish filters on matching pairs from filters applied after null extension, so moving a predicate between ON and WHERE can remove preserved rows. Key constraints constrain how many matches exist; without those constraints equal values do not identify unique source rows. These examples assume SQL bags and equality conditions with ordinary NULL behavior. Write the matching pairs first, add unmatched rows required by the join type, then apply later filters. This makes lost preservation and duplicate multiplication visible rather than hiding them in an intuitive diagram of overlapping sets.
''',
E('fundamental','Employee(id,name,manager) has (1,Ana,NULL),(2,Ben,1),(3,Cid,1). Self-join employee e to manager m on e.manager=m.id. List inner-join name pairs.',"Assign aliases::::e represents the employee role and m the manager role.~Find manager matches::::Ben and Cid each point to id 1, whose name is Ana.~Exclude unmatched role::::Ana's NULL manager does not equal a manager id under ordinary equality.",'Pairs (Ben,Ana) and (Cid,Ana).','Every output is supported by one concrete foreign-key-like reference, and the top-level manager has no matching parent role.'),
E('application','L IDs={1,2}; R rows={(1,active),(1,inactive)}. Compare LEFT JOIN with R.status=active in ON versus in WHERE.','Filter matching pairs in ON::::ID 1 matches only active; ID 2 has no qualifying match and is null-extended.~Filter after joining in WHERE::::The ordinary left join first adds ID 2 with NULL status, but NULL=active is UNKNOWN.~Compare preservation::::ON gives (1,active),(2,NULL); WHERE gives only (1,active).','ON preserves two rows; WHERE returns one.','The missing ID 2 row is explained by its post-join UNKNOWN predicate, not by absence of the left source row.'),
E('trap','A table has two rows with key value x; B has three rows with x. Neither key column is unique. Does their equality join produce one row because only one distinct value matches?','Retain source multiplicities::::Two separate A rows and three separate B rows remain distinct bag inputs.~Enumerate matching pairs::2\\cdot3=6::Each A row pairs with every B row carrying x.~Avoid implicit deduplication::::Equality join does not apply DISTINCT or invent a uniqueness constraint.','The join produces six rows.','Labeling A rows a1,a2 and B rows b1,b2,b3 yields six explicit combinations, independently checking the product.'))

P('8-queries-group-filter-trace','Grouping',r'''
A group is defined by equality of grouping keys among rows that survive earlier query stages. Aggregates summarize that actual input, including multiplicity introduced by joins. COUNT(*) counts group rows, COUNT(expression) counts nonnull expression results, and HAVING can reject a group after these values exist. Grouping multiple columns uses their combination, not an independent aggregation for each column. The examples assume ordinary SQL grouping and explicitly stated source rows, with no automatic DISTINCT. Make a group-to-row map before calculating, then test each group's aggregate predicate. This also exposes a common counting error after an outer join: one null-extended row represents zero matched children but still contributes one to COUNT(*).
''',
E('fundamental','Sales(region,product,amount) has (N,A,2),(N,A,3),(N,B,4),(S,A,5). Group by region,product and sum amount.','Build composite groups::::The distinct key pairs are (N,A),(N,B),(S,A).~Aggregate their rows::::(N,A) contains 2 and 3; the other groups contain one value each.~Emit summaries::::Results are (N,A,5),(N,B,4),(S,A,5).','Three groups with sums 5,4,5 respectively.','All group sums total 14, equal to 2+3+4+5 across the source.'),
E('application','Customer IDs 1 and 2 LEFT JOIN Order on customer ID. Only customer 1 has orders IDs 10 and 11. Group by customer and compare COUNT(*) with COUNT(Order.id).','Write joined groups::::Customer 1 has two matched rows; customer 2 has one null-extended row.~Count all rows::::COUNT(*) gives 2 for customer 1 and 1 for customer 2.~Count actual child identifiers::::COUNT(Order.id) ignores the null and gives 2 and 0.','Customer 1: (2,2); customer 2: (1,0) for the two counts.','Only two real orders exist, and child-identifier counts sum to two; COUNT(*) includes preservation metadata.'),
E('trap','Rows are (A,4),(A,6),(B,7). A student uses WHERE SUM(value)>8 before GROUP BY key. Correct the query and evaluate it.','Identify the unavailable value::::A row-level WHERE stage has no completed group sum.~Move the group predicate::::Use GROUP BY key HAVING SUM(value)>8.~Evaluate formed groups::::A sums to 10 and passes; B sums to 7 and fails.','The corrected grouped query returns A with sum 10.','Testing individual values>8 would return no rows and demonstrates that row filtering cannot replace aggregate filtering.'))

P('8-normalization-normalforms','Normal forms',r'''
Normal-form classification is a sequence of precise constraint tests, not a visual assessment of whether a table looks tidy. State atomicity first, find all candidate keys, mark prime attributes, and then examine partial dependencies and nonkey determinants. A schema can pass one test and fail the next; the first failing condition determines its highest form in this sequence. Dependencies inferred from the given set matter even if a violating arrow was not printed separately. The examples assume ordinary atomic relation attributes unless the prompt explicitly supplies a collection-valued field. Classification alone does not prove a proposed decomposition is lossless or preserves dependencies, so keep those additional properties as separate obligations rather than treating normalization as an automatic safe rewrite.
''',
E('fundamental','Atomic R(A,B,C) has only A→BC. Determine its highest form among 1NF,2NF,3NF,BCNF.','Find the key::A^+=ABC::A is the only candidate key because no dependency derives A.~Inspect nontrivial determinants::::Any determining set for B or C through the supplied dependency includes A.~Apply BCNF::::Every nontrivial determinant is therefore a superkey, satisfying the strongest listed form.','R is in BCNF.','There is no nonkey determinant supplying a nontrivial dependency, so weaker forms cannot be the limiting condition.'),
E('application','Atomic R(A,B,C,D) has AB→C and C→D only. Determine candidate key and highest listed form.','Find the necessary key::::A and B cannot be derived, while AB reaches C then D; the sole candidate key is AB.~Check partial dependencies::::Neither A nor B alone determines a nonprime attribute, so 2NF holds.~Find the stronger violation::::C→D has a non-superkey determinant and nonprime D, violating 3NF and BCNF.','Key AB; highest form 2NF.','C closure is CD, missing A and B, and D appears in no candidate key, supplying both parts of the 3NF violation.'),
E('trap','Person(id,phones) has unique id, but phones is explicitly a set of independently queried phone values within one cell under a model requiring atomic scalar fields. Does uniqueness make it BCNF?','Check the prerequisite::::The declared collection-valued cell violates the stipulated atomic-field requirement.~Separate key strength::::A unique identifier does not transform the collection into a scalar domain value.~Stop the classification::::The relation is not 1NF under this model, so cannot satisfy higher forms that require it.','It fails 1NF despite having a unique identifier.','Separating Phone(person_id,phone) into individual tuples addresses this representation issue; changing id alone does not.'))

P('8-normalization-fd-tuple-counterexample','Functional dependencies',r'''
A functional dependency expresses agreement propagation between tuples. It is directional: knowing a department identifies its manager does not imply a manager identifies just one department. Inference rules allow new dependencies without inspecting data, but each derivation must preserve the complete determinant requirement. For an instance test, group by the determinant and search for disagreeing dependent values; one such pair refutes the dependency. Passing an instance test cannot establish a universal schema rule. The examples assume classical dependencies without special SQL null complications. Contrast a tuple counterexample, a formal inference and a proposed reversal, because these are different proof tasks. Direction and joint determinants matter more than whether attribute labels sound like plausible business identifiers.
''',
E('fundamental','DepartmentManager(D,M) contains (10,Ana),(20,Ben),(10,Cid). Test D→M.','Group equal determinants::::Department 10 occurs with both Ana and Cid.~Compare dependent values::::Those two tuples agree on D but disagree on M.~Use the witness::::That pair violates the required agreement implication.','The instance violates D→M.',"Department 20's single row cannot repair the violating department-10 pair."),
E('application','Given X→Y and YZ→W, derive XZ→W using dependency inference.','Augment the first dependency::XZ\\to YZ::Add Z to both sides of X→Y.~Use the joint determinant::::The second dependency requires both Y and Z, now supplied by XZ.~Apply transitivity::XZ\\to W::Combining XZ→YZ and YZ→W proves the result.','XZ→W follows from the given dependencies.','Closure of XZ contains X,Z, then Y, then W, independently confirming the inference.'),
E('trap','R(A,B)={(1,x),(2,x)} satisfies A→B. Must B→A also hold?','Test the forward rule::::No two distinct rows share A, so the instance satisfies A→B.~Test the proposed reverse::::Both rows share B=x but have different A values.~Reject reversal::::The equal-B pair violates B→A.','No; dependencies cannot generally be reversed.','A many-to-one mapping is functional in one direction while this explicit pair disproves inverse functionality.'))

P('8-normalization-fds','Closure',r'''
Attribute closure is a fixed-point computation under a supplied dependency set. Begin with the attributes already known, and add a right side only when every attribute in its left side is known. Revisit dependencies after each expansion, because an arrow that was unusable initially may become applicable later. The computation stops when a full pass adds nothing. Closure tests superkeys, implication and redundancy; minimality requires additional subset checks. The examples assume ordinary set-valued attribute lists and no hidden dependencies. Maintain a written evolving set to avoid firing composite determinants too early or overlooking consequences of a newly added attribute. The order of valid additions can differ while the final least fixed point remains the same.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A,B,C,D,E), F={A→B,B→C,D→E}. Compute (AD)+.','Initialize known attributes::(AD)^+=\\{A,D\\}::The starting determinant knows both supplied attributes.~Apply immediately usable rules::::A adds B and D adds E, giving A,B,D,E.~Continue to a fixed point::::B then adds C; all attributes are known and no further expansion is needed.','(AD)+={A,B,C,D,E}.','The result contains every schema attribute, so AD is a superkey; removing D loses E and removing A loses B,C.'),
E('application','F={A→B,B→C,A→C}. Is A→C redundant? Compute closure after removing it.','Remove only the proposed arrow::::Use Fprime={A→B,B→C}.~Run the reduced closure::::Start A, add B via A→B, then C via B→C.~Compare the implication::::C remains in A closure, so the removed dependency is implied by the remainder.','A→C is redundant.','The retained dependencies are a subset of F and derive the removed arrow, establishing equivalence of the two FD sets.'),
E('trap','F contains AB→C only. A student starts with A and immediately adds C because A occurs in the determinant. Correct A+.','Initialize the exact set::A^+=\\{A\\}::B is not initially known.~Test the whole left side::::AB is not contained in {A}, so AB→C cannot fire.~Stop without additions::::No dependency applies; the fixed point remains {A}.','A+={A}; C cannot be inferred.','Starting with AB would permit C, highlighting why partial determinant matching is invalid.'))

P('8-normalization-2nf-composite-key','2NF',r'''
Second normal form focuses on nonprime facts depending on only part of a candidate key. It does not prohibit every dependency from a key subset, because a dependent prime attribute is outside its forbidden case. Alternate composite keys remain relevant even when a single-column key was chosen as primary. Find all keys and mark prime status before testing proper subsets; otherwise a convenient primary-key shortcut can produce a false classification. The examples assume 1NF and the exact listed dependencies. A violation identifies a repeated fact whose determinant can become a separate relation, but that split still needs losslessness and preservation proofs. Use a concrete attribute membership argument rather than the informal claim that a table with any singleton key is automatically safe.
''',
E('fundamental','Stock(W,P,City,Qty) has WP→Qty and W→City. W and P are underivable. Test 2NF.','Find the sole candidate key::::WP determines every attribute and both W and P are necessary.~Mark the dependent fact::::City is nonprime; W is a proper subset of WP.~Apply the 2NF rule::::W→City is a partial dependency of a nonprime attribute on the key.','Stock violates 2NF.','Two products in one warehouse repeat the same City fact, providing the associated update-anomaly intuition.'),
E('application','Atomic R(A,B,C,D) has A→BC,BC→A,B→D. A is primary key. Determine whether the singleton-primary-key shortcut establishes 2NF.','Find alternate candidates::::A closure is ABCD; BC also derives A then D, so A and BC are candidate keys.~Classify D::::D belongs to neither key and is nonprime.~Test the composite key::::B is a proper subset of BC and B→D, so 2NF fails despite the selected singleton primary key.','The relation is not 2NF; all candidate keys must be considered.','B closure is BD, so B is not itself a key; its dependency on nonprime D is genuinely partial.'),
E('trap','R(A,B,C) has A→B and B→A. Candidate keys are AC and BC. Does A→B violate 2NF because A is a proper part of AC?','Mark prime attributes::::A,B,C each belong to AC or BC.~Inspect the right side::::B is prime, so it is not a forbidden nonprime dependent attribute.~Apply the definition::::There is no nonprime attribute and therefore no 2NF partial-dependency violation.','The relation satisfies 2NF; this dependency does not violate it.','A is not a superkey, which may matter for BCNF, but 2NF uses a different right-side condition.'))

P('8-normalization-3nf-prime-exception','3NF',r'''
Third normal form balances dependency enforcement with removing nonkey dependencies of nonprime facts. Test each nontrivial single-attribute right side: a superkey determinant is acceptable, and a prime dependent attribute is also acceptable. The latter exception must use all candidate keys, even if the schema includes other nonprime attributes. A synthesis-style decomposition groups dependencies into relations and must include a key-containing component for the usual lossless construction. These examples assume atomic attributes and complete reasoning from the supplied dependencies. Distinguish classification from constructing a replacement schema: proving a violation does not select a unique decomposition. Show the determinant closure and prime membership explicitly, since a dependency chain alone is not a substitute for the formal rule.
''',
E('fundamental','Employee(E,D,City) has E→D and D→City. E is the only key. Test 3NF.','Classify the determinant::::D closure is D,City, not the complete schema, so D is not a superkey.~Classify the dependent::::City is absent from the sole key E and is nonprime.~Apply both alternatives::::D→City satisfies neither a superkey determinant nor a prime right side.','The relation fails 3NF.','Employee identifier E determining everything does not repair the separate nonkey dependency D→City.'),
E('application','R(A,B,C,D) has A→BC and C→D. Construct a dependency-preserving 3NF split and check losslessness.','Group dependency facts::::Use R1(A,B,C) for A→BC and R2(C,D) for C→D.~Check component keys::::A is a key in R1; C is a key in R2, so both components satisfy BCNF and hence 3NF.~Check the split::::Intersection C determines all of R2, proving losslessness; each original dependency is locally enforceable.','ABC and CD form a lossless dependency-preserving 3NF decomposition.','A full key A is contained in ABC, and the explicit binary intersection test independently supplies the recombination proof.'),
E('trap','R(A,B,C,D) has A→B,B→A,AC→D. Candidate keys AC and BC. Does nonkey A→B necessarily violate 3NF when D is nonprime?','Classify attributes::::A,B,C are prime; D is nonprime.~Inspect the relevant dependency::::A→B has prime B on its right side, so the 3NF exception applies.~Check remaining rules::::B→A also has a prime right side; AC→D has a key determinant. All given dependencies meet 3NF.','R is 3NF despite nonkey A→B; it fails BCNF.',"D's nonprime status concerns dependencies producing D, where AC is a key. It does not cancel B's prime status."))

P('8-normalization-bcnf-loss-preservation','BCNF',r'''
BCNF asks whether a determining set is a superkey, without a prime-attribute exception. A decomposition using a violating dependency isolates its repeated fact, but the resulting pieces can trade local enforcement for stronger normal form. Compute projected dependencies rather than merely copying arrows that fit syntactically, and examine each component's own keys. A binary split is lossless when the intersection determines one whole component under the original dependencies. These examples assume standard FD-based normalization and atomic attributes. A relation with several candidate keys is not automatically BCNF: a proper part of one key can determine an attribute without determining the complete schema. Keep the three claims—normal form, losslessness and preservation—independent during verification.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A,B,C) has A→B and B→A only. Determine BCNF using candidate keys AC and BC.','Compute A closure::A^+=AB::A lacks C and is not a superkey.~Identify the nontrivial arrow::::A→B has a nonkey determinant.~Apply BCNF directly::::The determinant requirement fails even though B is prime through candidate key BC.','R violates BCNF.','B→A supplies a second symmetric nonkey violation; prime status cannot rescue either one in BCNF.'),
E('application','R(A,B,C,D) has A→B,B→C,C→D. Decompose to BCNF by removing nonkey facts.','Separate C→D::::Split into CD and ABC; common C determines CD, so this split is lossless.~Separate B→C in ABC::::Split ABC into BC and AB; common B determines BC, again lossless.~Check local determinants::::Keys are C in CD, B in BC and A in AB. Each component has only key-based nontrivial dependencies.','BCNF components AB,BC,CD; sequentially lossless and dependency-preserving.','A→B,B→C,C→D each remains local, and both explicit intersection proofs establish recombination.'),
E('trap','R(A,B,C) has AB→C and C→B. Someone declares BCNF because every attribute belongs to a candidate key. What check refutes this?','Compute all key memberships::::AB and AC are candidate keys, so every attribute is indeed prime.~Inspect a determinant closure::C^+=BC::C does not determine A.~Apply the stronger requirement::::C→B is nontrivial with a non-superkey determinant, violating BCNF despite prime membership.','All-prime attributes suffice for this 3NF exception, but not BCNF.','The missing A in C closure is the independent witness that C is not a superkey.'))

P('8-normalization-decomposition','Lossless decomposition',r'''
A lossless decomposition must reconstruct every legal original relation exactly, rather than merely retaining every original tuple among extra combinations. Shared attributes supply join agreement, but only a dependency on a whole component prevents spurious combinations in a binary FD-based split. If the intersection determines neither component, construct two original tuples sharing the intersection and differing on both private sides; their projections can cross-pair. For more than two components, apply a valid sequential binary proof or a general chase rather than reusing one intersection test indiscriminately. The examples assume set projection and natural join over the shown schemas. Verify losslessness using both dependency closure and a concrete recombination witness where possible, separating it from the question of which dependencies can be enforced locally.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A,B,C) has B→A. Split into AB and BC. Prove or refute losslessness.','Identify the shared attributes::AB\\cap BC=\\{B\\}::B is the natural-join agreement column.~Compute the implication::::B→A plus reflexivity gives B→AB.~Use the binary criterion::::The intersection determines the whole AB component, so the split is lossless.','The decomposition is lossless.','For each B the AB projection supplies only its permitted A value, preventing spurious A/C cross-combinations.'),
E('application','R(A,B,C,D) has A→B and C→D. Split into AB and ACD, then split ACD into AC and CD. Check the final decomposition.','Prove the first split::::Intersection A determines AB through A→B.~Prove the second split::::Intersection C determines CD through C→D.~Compose the reconstruction::::Both binary operations are lossless, so joining AB,AC,CD reconstructs every legal original instance.','AB,AC,CD is a lossless decomposition.','AC retains the association between A and C; dropping that component would lose the cross-entity pairing information.'),
E('trap','With no nontrivial FDs, original R(A,B,C) contains (1,x,p),(2,x,q). Split into AB and BC and rejoin.','Project the original::::AB={(1,x),(2,x)} and BC={(x,p),(x,q)}.~Join on the shared B::::Both A values combine with both C values, producing four tuples.~Identify spurious tuples::::(1,x,q) and (2,x,p) were absent from the original; shared B determines neither side.','The split is lossy, adding two spurious tuples.','The reconstructed cardinality four exceeds original cardinality two, and the two new tuples explicitly witness the failure.'))

P('8-normalization-decomposition','Dependency preservation',r'''
Dependency preservation means enforcing constraints inside individual decomposed relations is sufficient to enforce every original dependency. Compute the dependencies projected onto each component, take their union, and test original arrows by closure under that union. A dependency need not appear verbatim inside one component to be preserved; a chain of local implications can enforce it indirectly. Conversely, a lossless join does not guarantee that separate component checks detect every cross-component violation. The examples assume complete projected FD reasoning, not merely selecting visually printed arrows. Build a small legal set of component instances to refute preservation when closure fails. That counterexample makes the enforcement cost concrete: detecting the missing constraint requires combining facts from more than one component.
''',
E('fundamental','R(A,B,C) has A→B,B→C. Split into AB and BC. Check preservation of both dependencies.','Project onto AB::::A→B can be checked using only AB tuples.~Project onto BC::::B→C can be checked using only BC tuples.~Combine enforcement::::The union contains both original arrows and therefore their implied A→C.','The split preserves the original dependency set.','Closure of A under the local union is ABC, independently verifying the implied chain.'),
E('application','F={A→B,B→C,A→C}. Components AB and BC do not contain AC. Is the explicitly listed A→C nevertheless preserved?','Collect local rules::::AB enforces A→B and BC enforces B→C.~Compute the relevant closure::::Starting from A, add B then C under those local rules.~Check the original arrow::::C is in A closure, so A→C follows even without a component containing both attributes.','Yes; preservation can be indirect through local dependency inference.','Transitivity gives A→C, so the missing AC schema is not evidence of failed preservation.'),
E('trap','R(A,B,C) has A→B and B→C. Split into AB and AC. Is B→C preserved merely because the split is lossless?','Establish the distinct property::::A determines AB, so the shared-A binary split is lossless.~Collect projected constraints::::AB has A→B; AC has implied A→C. Starting from B adds nothing.~Construct a violation::::AB={(1,b),(2,b)} and AC={(1,c1),(2,c2)} satisfy locals but their join gives one B with different C values.','The split is lossless but does not preserve B→C.','The explicit component instances pass every stated local rule and yield a concrete B→C violation after joining.'))

P('8-storage-index-file-organization-cost','File organization',r'''
File organization is a physical arrangement of records, and its benefits depend on the operation. A heap avoids maintaining key order but can require inspecting every page for an absent key. A rigid sorted file supports ordered access while insertion may shift records through subsequent pages. A secondary index can provide an ordered search route without making the underlying heap clustered. These examples assume explicit page packing and no compression, caching or hidden free slots. Calculate pages from record capacity, then trace the particular operation's data-page effects. Separate lookup assistance from physical adjacency: neighboring key values can still require different scattered data pages. This makes the maintenance cost and query cost of a layout visible rather than declaring one organization universally superior.
''',
E('fundamental','An unindexed heap holds 950 records, 20 per page, tightly packed. Prove an equality target absent. How many data pages must be read?','Count allocated pages::N=\\lceil950/20\\rceil=48::The final page is partially occupied.~Use the unordered model::::No key ordering or index permits eliminating an unread page.~Complete the absence check::::Read all 48 pages to inspect every possible record location.','48 data-page reads in the uncached worst case.','47 pages hold only 940 records, so a 48th page is necessary despite containing just ten records.'),
E('application','Sorted fixed pages hold two records each: [10,20],[30,40],[50,60], all full. Maintain left-packed order while inserting 25. Compare a heap append with a new page.','Keep the sorted sequence::::The final record sequence is 10,20,25,30,40,50,60.~Repack affected pages::::Page 1 stays [10,20]; write pages 2=[25,30], 3=[40,50], and new page 4=[60].~Compare heap insertion::::An unordered heap can write new page 4=[25] without shifting existing records, under the stipulated no-free-slot layout.','The sorted layout writes three data pages; the heap append writes one.','Both store seven records in four pages; only the ordering requirement changes which existing pages must be rewritten.'),
E('trap','A heap has page P1 keys [1,100] and P2 keys [2,99]. A secondary ordered index locates keys 1 and 2. Does sorted index order imply one clustered data-page read for range [1,2]?','Locate matching keys::::The index identifies keys 1 and 2 in order.~Follow their record locations::::Key 1 lies in P1 and key 2 in P2.~Count physical pages::::Both distinct heap pages must be fetched; the index did not reorder their records.','Two data pages, plus any specified index traversal.','The supplied physical layout directly separates the two neighboring keys, refuting automatic clustering.'))

P('8-storage-index-files','Indexing',r'''
An index trades maintained metadata for selective access to data locations. Dense and sparse representations offer different pointer granularity; sparsity usually relies on an ordered underlying file to search inside a candidate block. Traversal cost is only part of a query's I/O: qualifying records may require many data-page fetches, particularly for an unclustered index. A full scan can therefore beat indexed access when selectivity is poor. These examples assume explicit cache conditions, no covering payload unless stated, and distinct data pages counted once. Write the traversal path and the set of data pages separately, then add them. Do not infer one data access per qualifying row when several rows share a page, or assume every useful index is optimal for every query.
''',
E('fundamental','A sorted file has 12 distinct-key records across four pages, three per page. Compare one-entry-per-record dense indexing with one-entry-per-page sparse indexing.','Count dense entries::::The stipulated dense representation needs one entry for each of 12 records.~Count sparse entries::::The sparse representation needs the first-key pointer for each of four pages.~Interpret lookup granularity::::Dense entries identify records; sparse lookup identifies a candidate ordered page that must be searched.','Dense: 12 entries; sparse: 4 entries.','Three records per page times four pages equals twelve, explaining the entry-count ratio without changing payload size.'),
E('application','An uncached unclustered index lookup visits three index pages and returns five records on data pages [7,9,7,20,25]. Count total reads with each fetched page retained.','Count traversal::::Three distinct index pages are required.~Deduplicate payload pages::::The five records occupy four distinct pages: 7,9,20,25.~Combine access classes::I/O=3+4=7::The second record on page 7 does not cause another retained-page read.','Seven page reads.','A per-record addition would give eight and double-count page 7; the actual distinct-page set supplies the check.'),
E('trap','A query needs every row of a 100-page table. A noncovering index requires three uncached traversal pages then all 100 data pages. Is index use necessarily faster than a scan?','Count the scan::::A full table scan needs 100 data-page reads in this model.~Count indexed retrieval::::Traversal plus all payload pages needs 3+100=103 reads.~Compare selectivity benefit::::No data page is avoided, so the extra metadata work provides no I/O saving.','The scan has lower modeled I/O: 100 versus 103 reads.','An index benefit depends on avoiding data work or covering the query; neither is true in the supplied scenario.'))

P('8-storage-index-btree-internal-match','B trees',r'''
A B-tree keeps search height bounded by packing several ordered keys in each node and enforcing occupancy. Page size determines maximum children through both pointer and separator costs, while insertion may split a full node and promote its median. Promotion changes the parent and can create a new root, increasing every leaf's depth together. Ordinary B-tree records may live internally, so deletion and traversal should not assume that internal keys are merely duplicated routing labels. The examples state maximum keys or minimum degree explicitly because order terminology varies. Count keys and children separately: an internal node with f children contains f−1 separators, and the root has different minimum occupancy rules from ordinary nodes.
''',
E('fundamental','An internal page has 256 usable bytes, eight-byte child pointers and eight-byte keys. Find maximum fanout, ignoring all other overhead.','Write the node layout::8f+8(f-1)\\le256::An internal node uses f pointers and f−1 keys.~Solve the capacity bound::16f\\le264::Integer fanout is at most 16.~Verify adjacent choices::::f=16 uses 248 bytes; f=17 uses 264 and does not fit.','Maximum fanout 16.','Sixteen children require fifteen keys, not sixteen; the explicit fit/nonfit test checks the rounding.'),
E('application','A B-tree has minimum degree two, maximum three keys per node. Its root is a full leaf [10,20,30]. Insert 40 using split-before-descent.','Split the full root::::Promote median 20 into a new root; create leaves [10] and [30].~Choose the correct child::::40 exceeds 20 and enters the right leaf.~Insert without overflow::::Right leaf becomes [30,40], within the three-key maximum.','Root [20], children [10] and [30,40]; leaf depth increases from zero to one.','All four keys appear once and both children meet minimum one-key occupancy; their ranges agree with separator 20.'),
E('trap','For a B-tree with maximum four children per ordinary internal node and minimum two, a nonleaf root has one key and two children. Is it underfull because it lacks two keys?','Relate keys and children::::Two child pointers require one separator key.~Apply the root exception::::A nonleaf root may have two children under the stated convention.~Check the claimed bound::::Minimum children two does not mean minimum keys two.','The root is valid with one key and two children.','For a node with c children the separator count is c−1, so the one-key layout matches two children exactly.'))

P('8-storage-index-bplus-linked-range','B+ trees',r'''
B+ trees separate routing from leaf record storage. When a leaf splits, the parent separator commonly copies the new right leaf's first key rather than removing that record from the leaf. Search equality follows the specified separator convention to a leaf, and range continuation uses leaf links. Leaf capacity and internal capacity differ because leaves contain record references and a next-leaf pointer rather than child arrays. These examples use explicit layouts and rightward routing at separator equality. Count result records at leaves only, and count data-page fetches separately if leaves store pointers instead of payload. A separator appearing both internally and in a leaf represents one record plus routing information, not two copies to emit in a query result.
''',
E('fundamental','A B+ root separator is 40; keys <40 go left and >=40 right. Left leaf=[10,20], right leaf=[40,50]. Locate equality key 40 with uncached pages.','Read the root::::Compare 40 to separator 40.~Apply the routing convention::::Equality follows the right child, not the left interval.~Read the record leaf::::The right leaf contains key 40, completing the lookup after two page reads.','Read root and right leaf; key 40 is found with two index-page reads.','The separator routes equality but does not itself contain the result record under the B+ representation.'),
E('application','A B+ leaf holds at most three entries. Leaf [10,20,30] receives 25; split the four entries evenly and update its parent using first-right-key separator.','Sort the overflow::::The entries become [10,20,25,30].~Split the payload::::Left leaf becomes [10,20]; right leaf becomes [25,30], with the leaf link updated.~Copy the routing key::::Insert separator 25 into the parent; keep record 25 in the right leaf.','Leaves [10,20] and [25,30], parent separator 25.','Each leaf has two entries within capacity three, and all four record entries remain at leaf level.'),
E('trap','A B+ internal page contains separator 30 and its linked leaves contain records 10,20,30,40. An ordered full scan counts the separator as another record. Correct its count.','Identify metadata role::::Internal 30 routes searches and is not a separate stored record.~Visit the leaves::::The four leaf records are 10,20,30,40.~Count results once::::Emit those four records without adding the internal copy.','Four records, not five.','The leaf sequence contains exactly one record for each supplied key; structural routing copies do not add payload multiplicity.'))

P('8-transactions-acid','Transactions',r'''
A transaction has an execution lifetime and a single commit-or-abort outcome under the stated database contract. Operations may temporarily alter private or protected state before commit, while rollback restores the logical effects required by atomicity. Savepoints can undo a suffix without ending the whole transaction. A commit does not correct an invalid application decision automatically, and separate statements are not one atomic unit merely because they run consecutively. These examples assume transactional storage and explicit boundaries, rather than external irreversible side effects. Trace the state after each operation and mark which changes survive the final outcome. This distinguishes transient values, savepoint rollback and durable committed values, and exposes algorithms that accidentally split one business operation across two independent commits.
''',
E('fundamental','A transaction starts with x=10, writes x=15, then aborts. No other transaction changes x. What survives?',"Record the original state::::The pretransaction logical value is 10.~Apply the tentative update::::The transaction's own work changes x to 15 before its outcome.~Execute abort recovery::::Undo the transaction's effect, restoring the original value 10.",'After abort, x=10.','Only the aborting transaction changed x, so retaining 15 would leave an uncommitted effect.'),
E('application','x=10. Begin a transaction, set x=12, create savepoint S, set x=20, roll back to S, then commit. Trace final x.','Establish the savepoint state::::At S, x=12; the earlier update belongs to the still-active transaction.~Undo only the suffix::::Rollback to S removes the later x=20 update, returning x to 12.~Commit remaining work::::Commit retains the pre-savepoint change to 12.','Final committed x=12.','Rollback to a savepoint differs from aborting the entire transaction, which would have restored 10.'),
E('trap','A transfer commits its debit in transaction T1, then its credit in separate T2. A crash occurs before T2 begins. Are the two commits an atomic transfer?',"Identify the boundaries::::T1's debit has already committed as its own unit.~Locate the failure::::T2 never performs the credit, so there is no transaction containing both actions.~Inspect surviving state::::The committed debit remains without its intended credit; per-transaction atomicity cannot join separate boundaries.",'No; the business transfer is split and can leave a partial result.','Putting debit and credit inside one transactional unit supplies the all-or-nothing boundary that is missing here.'))

P('8-transactions-lock-compatibility-wait','Concurrency control',r'''
Concurrency control protects acceptable interleavings, and different protocols choose different points to reject or delay conflicting work. Locks block incompatible operations before they execute; timestamp rules can reject an operation that is too old; optimistic validation can discover an invalid read set before commit. Track successful actions separately from requested actions, since a blocked write has not changed data. Shared locks permit multiple readers but conflict with a writer, and upgrades require checking all remaining readers. These examples state the lock compatibility or validation rule explicitly rather than assuming every implementation uses the same policy. Show a state snapshot of owners, readers, observed versions and pending operations, then derive the permitted next action from that state.
''',
E('fundamental','T1 and T2 hold shared locks on X. T3 requests an exclusive lock on X. T1 releases, while T2 keeps reading. Can T3 proceed?',"Inspect compatibility::::Exclusive X conflicts with each existing shared holder.~Remove one blocker::::T1's release removes its shared lock but T2 remains.~Keep the writer waiting::::T3 can acquire only after all conflicting shared holders release.",'T3 remains blocked until T2 also releases.','An exclusive owner cannot coexist with even one other shared owner; counting the remaining reader supplies the decision.'),
E('application','An optimistic transaction reads X=5 at version 7. Another transaction commits X=9 and increments its version to 8. Validation requires every read version unchanged. May the first commit?',"Record the read snapshot::::The transaction's read set contains X at version 7.~Inspect current metadata::::Committed X now has version 8.~Apply validation::::Version mismatch violates the unchanged-read rule, so abort or retry before committing.",'The first transaction fails validation and must retry or abort.','The check compares versions, not whether its final write happens to look reasonable; the committed update supplies an explicit conflict witness.'),
E('trap','T1 holds shared X and requests upgrade to exclusive X while T2 still holds shared X. Does ownership of one shared lock make the upgrade immediately legal?',"Distinguish modes::::T1's shared permission permits reads, not an exclusive write.~Check other owners::::T2's shared lock conflicts with T1's requested exclusive mode.~Delay the upgrade::::T1 waits under the specified compatibility rule until the other shared holder leaves.",'The upgrade must wait; holding a shared lock is not exclusive ownership.','If T2 releases, the incompatible other-reader set becomes empty and the upgrade can then be granted.'))

P('8-transactions-acid-transfer-guarantees','ACID',r'''
ACID claims should be tested at concrete failure boundaries. Atomicity concerns all effects of one incomplete unit, durability concerns effects after an acknowledged commit, consistency depends on declared invariants and correct logic, and isolation governs concurrent observations. Logging rules support recovery but do not by themselves make faulty business logic correct. A valid committed transaction can still be reported incorrectly to a concurrent reader under weak isolation. These examples assume explicit transactional guarantees, protected database state, and the named failure timing. Identify the exact observation or surviving state that challenges a property, rather than answering every scenario with the whole acronym. Distinguishing precommit undo from postcommit preservation is especially useful because the two require opposite recovery outcomes.
''',
E('fundamental','An atomic transaction inserts header H and two required detail rows, then aborts before completion. What should the final database contain from that transaction?','Identify one unit::::Header and both details belong to the same transaction.~Locate the abort boundary::::No successful commit occurred.~Apply all-or-nothing recovery::::Remove every effect of this unit, including any header or detail already inserted.',"None of that transaction's inserted rows survive.",'Leaving H alone would expose a partial unit; durability does not protect an uncommitted insertion.'),
E('application','A write-ahead log must durably store update log records before their data pages, and the commit record before acknowledging commit. Data pages are still old at a crash after acknowledgment. Is recovery allowed to forget the update?','Use the ordering rule::::The acknowledged commit guarantees durable commit and preceding update records.~Inspect old data pages::::Old pages indicate pending propagation, not absence of a committed transaction.~Redo committed work::::Recovery uses durable log information to restore the committed effects.','No; durability requires recovering the acknowledged committed update.','The log survives by the explicit ordering rule, supplying recovery information even though data-page flushing lagged.'),
E('trap','A database declares a nonnegative account-balance invariant, but its check is absent in this hypothetical schema. Application logic atomically commits a negative balance. Does atomicity imply consistency?','Inspect the unit outcome::::All writes can succeed together, so atomicity alone may hold.~Test the invariant::::A negative committed balance violates the declared nonnegative rule.~Separate guarantees::::All-or-nothing execution does not prove that the committed logic preserves required invariants.','No; atomicity cannot establish consistency of incorrect or unenforced logic.','Undoing a partial write and validating a completed value are different checks; the final negative state is the explicit consistency violation.'))

P('8-transactions-schedules','Serializability',r'''
Serializability compares an interleaved schedule with a possible serial execution while preserving the relevant observations or conflict orders. Conflict serializability uses same-item pairs containing a write and tests the resulting directed precedence graph. Read/read pairs add no edge, and commit placement belongs to recovery analysis rather than the conflict graph alone. View serializability preserves read sources and final writers and can differ when blind writes exist. The examples specify which equivalence is being tested. Build edges from actual ordered operations before selecting a serial order, and separately track any read of uncommitted data. Acyclic conflict order can coexist with unsafe commit order, so do not let one successful graph test answer every property of the schedule.
''',
E('fundamental','Schedule R1(X),R2(X),W1(Y),R2(Y). Build the conflict graph and a serial witness.','Ignore the read/read pair::::R1(X) and R2(X) do not conflict.~Add the Y dependency::::W1(Y) before R2(Y) adds T1→T2.~Topologically order the graph::::There is no reverse edge, so serial T1 then T2 respects every conflict.','Conflict serializable with witness T1,T2.','Only one edge exists; the same-item X operations contain no write and cannot create a cycle.'),
E('application','Schedule W1(X),W2(X),W3(Y),R2(Y). Find all required ordering edges and a serial order.','Inspect X writes::::W1(X) before W2(X) gives T1→T2.~Inspect Y conflict::::W3(Y) before R2(Y) gives T3→T2.~Choose a topological order::::T1,T3,T2 works, as does T3,T1,T2, because T1 and T3 have no edge between them.','Edges T1→T2 and T3→T2; two valid orders are T1,T3,T2 and T3,T1,T2.','Both proposed orders place T2 after its two predecessors, and no other same-item cross-transaction conflict exists.'),
E('trap','Schedule W1(X),R2(X),C2,A1. Its conflict graph is acyclic. Does that ensure recoverability?',"Check the graph::::The write-read conflict gives only T1→T2, so conflict serializability holds.~Track the read source::::T2 reads T1's uncommitted X and commits before T1 commits.~Inspect the abort::::T1 aborts after T2's commit, leaving T2 committed with a value from an aborted transaction.",'Conflict serializable but not recoverable.','Recoverability requires the read-source writer to commit before the reader; here the writer never commits.'))

P('8-transactions-locking','Two phase locking',r'''
Two-phase locking constrains each transaction's lock history, including upgrades. All acquisitions and promotions belong before the first release or demotion; later operations cannot start another growing phase. The lock point, its final acquisition, provides a serialization-order argument under the ordinary compatible-lock model. Basic 2PL does not require every lock to be held until commit, while strict 2PL specifically retains exclusive locks through completion. Holding shared and exclusive locks to the end is a stronger convention often called rigorous. These examples state mode changes explicitly, so a downgrade can be recognized as releasing exclusive permission. Verify phase order independently from compatibility and recovery: a legal acquisition phase can still block, and an early release may satisfy basic 2PL while violating strictness.
''',
E('fundamental','T acquires shared X, upgrades to exclusive X, acquires shared Y, then releases X and Y. Does it obey basic 2PL?','List acquisitions::::Shared X, upgrade to exclusive X, and shared Y all occur before any release.~Locate the lock point::::The acquisition of Y is the final acquisition and ends the growing work.~Inspect the shrinking phase::::Only releases follow, so there is no later acquisition.','The lock history obeys basic 2PL.','An upgrade counts as acquisition, but it is legal here because it occurs before the first release.'),
E('application','T acquires exclusive X and shared Y, writes X, releases Y, commits, then releases X. Classify basic and strict 2PL.','Check the two phases::::Both acquisitions precede the first release, and no new lock is acquired afterward.~Check exclusive retention::::Exclusive X remains held through commit, protecting the uncommitted write.~Check shared retention::::Shared Y is released early, allowed by strict 2PL under the stated exclusive-lock convention but not rigorous all-lock retention.','It follows basic and strict 2PL, but not rigorous retention of all locks.',"The early shared release does not expose X's uncommitted write because exclusive X remains owned until commit."),
E('trap','T acquires exclusive X, downgrades it to shared X, then acquires shared Y. A student says no lock was fully unlocked, so 2PL holds. Is that correct?','Interpret the downgrade::::Exclusive-to-shared removes exclusive permission and counts as a release/demotion.~Mark the phase boundary::::The downgrade begins the shrinking phase.~Inspect later acquisition::::Acquiring shared Y afterward starts new growing work and violates 2PL.','The history violates basic 2PL.','A partial mode release still ends the acquisition phase; complete unlock is not required to trigger the rule.'))
